tel 1.0

base honeycomb
import soundness

info   HTML as typed, immutable values: built from tags, parsed from text, matched, and rendered
detail
    _Honeycomb_ represents HTML as a tree of immutable values whose types carry what the HTML
    specification says about them. An element is built by applying a tag to its attributes
    and its children, and the construction is checked as the code compiles: a `<br>` given
    children, a `<p>` given a `src` attribute, or an `<li>` placed in a `<div>` is a compile
    error rather than malformed output. The same values are read back from text with
    `read[Html]` and `load[Html]`, matched with the `h"…"` interpolator as a pattern, joined
    and transformed with `+`, `^+`, `+^` and attribute assignment, and rendered with `show` or
    streamed from a `Document[Html]`.

    The central type is `Html`: a node or a sequence of nodes. Its companion holds the node
    kinds — `Html.Element`, `Html.Text`, `Html.Comment`, `Html.Doctype`, and `Html.Fragment`
    for a sequence — together with every given which parses, renders and converts HTML, the
    parser's `Html.Issue` and `Html.Position`, and the `Html.Path` which locates an element in
    a document loaded under `parsing.trackPositions`. A value's static type can say far more
    than `Html`: `Html of "br"` records the tag, `Html of Flow` a content category, `Html.Element
    of "ul" over "li"` what the element may contain, and `Html in Whatwg` which specification
    it conforms to.

    The vocabulary comes from a **DOM**, a `Dom` value chosen by importing one of the
    `htmlDoms` givens by name. `Whatwg` is the WHATWG HTML Living Standard — roughly 110 tag
    objects (`Div`, `P`, `Img`, …) in its companion, each typed with the children and
    attributes it admits, the attribute types (`Whatwg.Url`, `Whatwg.PositiveInt`, …), the
    content categories (`Flow`, `Phrasing`, `Metadata`, …) and the named character references
    — and `Html4Transitional` is the HTML 4.01 Transitional dialect, with its deprecated
    presentational tags and attributes. The `Tag` class is what every tag object is; `Attribute`,
    `Attributive` and `Unattributive` type each attribute's value; `Renderable` lets a value of
    any type stand where HTML is expected.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import htmlDoms.whatwg
    import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import attributives.textAttributive

    val page = Html(Head(Title("Hello")), Body(P("Hello world"), Img(src = t"dog.jpg")))
    page.show                                   // t"<html><head>…</head><body>…</body></html>"
    h"<ol><li>Alpha<li>Beta</ol>" == Ol(Li("Alpha"), Li("Beta"))
    t"<p>Hello <em>world</em></p>".read[Html of "p"]
    t"<html><body><p>Hi</p></body></html>".load[Html]
    ```

    Reading needs a `Dom` and, unless `recoveries.permissiveRecovery` is in scope, a
    `Tactic[Parse.Error]`; a parse failure is `Parse.Error` (error 594), whose position is an
    `Html.Position` and whose issue an `Html.Issue`. `strategies.throwUnsafely` throws it.

    The library is one source set, `honeycomb.core`, with no platform-specific component. The
    choice packages are imported by name, since `import soundness.*` does not import givens:

    - `htmlDoms` — `whatwg` and `html4Transitional`, the vocabulary a parse and a tag
      application use.
    - `formatting` — `indentedHtmlFormatting` (the default) and `compactHtmlFormatting`, how
      a `Document[Html]` is laid out when streamed.
    - `attributives` — `textAttributive`, which makes a `Text` acceptable for every
      attribute, whatever its declared type.
    - `stylesheets` — `uncheckedClasses`, which lets `P.info(…)` name any CSS class.
    - `recoveries` — `permissiveRecovery`, which selects the recovering parser.
    - `construables` — `htmlConstruable`, which construes a `text/html` body as a
      `Document[Html]`.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and each choice package merges with
    the umbrella's package of the same name. The node types are reached as `Html.Element`,
    `Html.Fragment` and so on: `import Html.*` is discouraged, since it would shadow
    `anticipation.Text` with `Html.Text`.

refer honeycomb.Html
refer honeycomb.Whatwg
refer honeycomb.Tag
refer honeycomb.Renderable
refer honeycomb.htmlDoms

entry .Html   an HTML value: one node or a sequence of nodes, typed by tag, content and specification
  detail
      `Html` is the sealed trait every HTML value conforms to. A value is either an
      `Html.Node` — exactly one `Html.Element`, `Html.Text`, `Html.Comment` or `Html.Doctype` —
      or an `Html.Fragment`, a sequence of nodes with no single parent. Because HTML is a text
      format, one node is rarely a necessary constraint, so `Html` admits sequences everywhere
      and `Html.Node` marks the few places where a single node is required. A fragment is
      unwrapped when supplied as children, and a fragment of one node equals that node.

      Three type members refine a value: `Topic`, the tag name (`Html of "br"`), or a union of
      names, or a content category such as `Flow` or `Phrasing` from the DOM in force;
      `Transport`, what the value may contain (`Html.Element of "ul" over "li"`); and `Form`,
      the `Dom` it conforms to (`Html in Whatwg`). The library uses this precision internally;
      applications rarely need more than `Html of Flow`.

      The object `Html` is itself the `<html>` tag — a `Tag.Container` admitting `head` and
      `body` — so a whole page is `Html(Head(…), Body(…))`, and `Html.doctype` is the
      `<!DOCTYPE html>` header. Its companion also holds every given which parses, renders
      and converts HTML: text and strings convert to `Html.Text` children, a `Renderable`
      value converts to the HTML it renders, and the `Aggregable`, `Loadable`, `Streamable`,
      `Showable` and `Inspectable` instances are what `read[Html]`, `load[Html]`,
      `document.stream[Text]`, `show` and `inspect` resolve.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A bare element
        code
            import soundness.*
            import htmlDoms.whatwg
            import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
            import attributives.textAttributive

            val image = Img(src = t"puppy.jpg")                 // Html.Element of "img"
      frame
        label An attribute assigned, giving a new value
        code
            val described = image.alt = t"A young dog"          // <img src="puppy.jpg" alt="A young dog">
      frame
        label Children applied to a container
        code
            val figure = Figure(described, Figcaption("Puppy"))
      frame
        label A child added at the start, and one at the end
        code
            val section = (Div(figure) ^+ H1("Gallery")) +^ P("More to come")
      frame
        label Two values joined into a fragment
        code
            val content = section + Hr                         // Html.Fragment of "div" | "hr"
      frame
        label The page, rendered
        code
            Html(Head(Title("Gallery")), Body(content)).show
      ##
      ```

      Every node is immutable: `image` is unchanged by the assignment, and `described` is a
      new element. Equality is structural: two elements are equal when their labels, their
      attributes (in any order), their children and their foreignness are, and a comment, a
      text or a doctype equals a fragment holding only it.

      Reading HTML needs a `Dom` in scope (`htmlDoms.whatwg`) and a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`,
      unless `recoveries.permissiveRecovery` selects the recovering parser, which needs no
      tactic. `read[Html]` accepts a fragment of any nodes; `read[Html of "ul"]` accepts only
      the tags its type names, which also guides the parser's inference of omitted tags; and
      `load[Html]` reads a whole document with its doctype as a `Document[Html]`, whose
      `metadata` is an `Html.Provenance`. `show` renders any node compactly against the
      WHATWG DOM; a `Document[Html]` streams through its own DOM, laid out by the
      `Html.Formatting` in scope.
  refer honeycomb.Whatwg
  refer honeycomb.Tag
  refer honeycomb.Renderable
  refer honeycomb.h
  refer honeycomb.Attributes
  entry .Formatting   how a streamed `Document[Html]` is laid out: indented one element per line, or compact
    detail
        `Html.Formatting` is honeycomb's `zephyrine.Formatting`, with one parameter:
        `indented`. When it is `true` (the companion's `default`, and
        `formatting.indentedHtmlFormatting`), elements whose DOM mode is `Html.Mode.Whitespace`
        — `html`, `head`, `body`, `ul`, `table` and the like, where text between children is
        insignificant — are written one per line, indented two spaces per level, to a depth
        of 32; when `false` (`formatting.compactHtmlFormatting`), the whole document is one
        line. It applies only to `Html.streamable`, the `Document[Html]` stream: `show` of a
        bare node is always compact.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val compact: Html.Formatting = Html.Formatting(indented = false)
        ```
    entry .apply   a formatting with the given indentation setting
    entry .default   the companion's given: indented
        In implicit scope, so a `Document[Html]` streams indented unless
        `formatting.compactHtmlFormatting` is imported, which, being lexically scoped,
        outranks it.
    entry #indented   whether whitespace-mode elements are laid out one per indented line
  entry .Integral   a marker trait for an integer-valued attribute type
      One of three marker traits in the companion (`Integral`, `Decimal`, `Id`), of the kind
      `Whatwg` declares for its attribute value types; the WHATWG attributes use
      `Whatwg.Integral`, `Whatwg.Decimal` and `Whatwg.Id` rather than these.
  entry .Decimal   a marker trait for a decimal-valued attribute type
      See `Html.Integral`; the WHATWG attributes use `Whatwg.Decimal`.
  entry .Id   a marker trait for an identifier-valued attribute type
      See `Html.Integral`; the WHATWG attributes use `Whatwg.Id`.
  entry .media   a `Document[Html]` has the media type `text/html; charset=UTF-8`
      The `Media` given which lets a `Document[Html]` be served as an HTTP response body, or
      sent as a request body, with the right `Content-Type`. It is in implicit scope.
  entry .doctype   the `<!DOCTYPE html>` header, as `Html.Doctype(t"html")`
  entry .nodes   flattens a `List[Html]` to an `Array[Html.Node]`, unwrapping fragments
      An extension on `List[Html]`: each `Html.Fragment` contributes its nodes and each node
      itself, so the result is the flat sequence a parent's children array holds. The tags'
      `apply` and `Html.Fragment.apply` use it, which is why a fragment supplied as a child is
      never nested.
  entry .interpolator   the given behind `h"…"`: parses the literal as the code compiles and splices nodes
      The `Interpolable` instance the `h` interpolator resolves. Its `interpolate` is a
      macro, `internal.interpolator`, which parses the parts against the WHATWG DOM, reports
      any `Html.Issue` at the literal, and instantiates the nodes directly at runtime, so the
      result costs no parsing. See `honeycomb.h`.
  entry .extrapolator   the given behind `h"…"` as a pattern: matches a scrutinee against the literal
      The `Extrapolable` instance which makes `case h"<li>$value</li>"` a pattern. Its
      `extrapolate` is a macro, `internal.extractor`, which parses the pattern as real HTML
      against the DOM — so a pattern written with closing tags matches input written without
      — and binds each hole to the text, attribute value, attribute map, element or child
      content at that position. See `honeycomb.h`.
  entry .addable   `left + right`: two HTML values joined into a fragment, typed by both topics
      The `Addable` given: `H1("title") + P("Hello")` is an `Html.Fragment of "h1" | "p" in
      Whatwg`, holding the nodes of both sides, flattened. Both sides must be in the same
      `Dom`.
  entry .strictAggregable   `read[Html of content]`: parses text as the tags the type names, failing on any error
      The `Aggregable` given for `read[Html of "ul"]`, `read[Html of "ul" | "ol"]`,
      `read[Html of Flow]` and any other union of tag names: the parser accepts only the
      named tags at the top level, which also guides its inference of omitted tags, so
      `<p>Hello</p>` read as `Html of "html"` gains the missing `<html>` and `<body>`. The
      result is a single `Html.Element`, `Html.Text` or `Html.Comment` when the source
      yields one node, and otherwise an `Html.Fragment` (empty, for empty text).

      It needs a `Dom` and a `Tactic[Parse.Error]` in scope, and resolves only when no
      `Html.Recovery.Permissive` is in scope: `recoveries.permissiveRecovery` switches to
      `Html.permissiveAggregable`. Every `Html.Issue` is raised through the tactic — the
      parser recovers from some, such as an inadmissible tag, after `raise`, and aborts on
      others.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"<ul><li>First<li>Second</ul>".read[Html of Flow] == Ul(Li("First"), Li("Second"))
      t"<div>a</div><p>b</p>".read[Html of Flow] == Html.Fragment(Div("a"), P("b"))
      ```
  entry .strictAggregable2   `read[Html]`: parses text as any nodes the DOM defines, failing on any error
      As `Html.strictAggregable`, with every element of the `Dom` admitted at the top level
      and no doctype accepted. Needs a `Dom` and a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`, and yields to
      `Html.permissiveAggregable2` when `Html.Recovery.Permissive` is in scope.
  entry .strictLoadable   `load[Html]`: reads a whole document as a `Document[Html]`, failing on any error
      The `Loadable` given: the source is parsed as a document — a doctype is accepted, the
      root is `<html>`, and omitted `<html>`, `<head>` and `<body>` are inferred — and the
      result is a `Document[Html]` whose `root` is the `<html>` element and whose `metadata`
      is an `Html.Provenance` holding the `Dom`. A source with no single root raises
      `Parse.Error` with `Html.Issue.BadDocument`.

      It needs a `Dom`, a `Tactic[Parse.Error]` and a `PositionTracking` (the default is
      `Off`; `import parsing.trackPositions` turns it on, and the document then records the
      source position of every element and attribute, for `document.locate(path)` with an
      `Html.Path`). It resolves only when no `Html.Recovery.Permissive` is in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val document: Document[Html] = t"<title>Hi</title><p>Hello</p>".load[Html]
      document.root == Html(Head(Title("Hi")), Body(P("Hello")))
      document.metadata.dom == whatwg
      ```
  entry .permissiveAggregable   `read[Html of content]` under `recoveries.permissiveRecovery`: recovers, never raises
      The recovering counterpart of `Html.strictAggregable`, selected by bringing an
      `Html.Recovery.Permissive` into scope (`import recoveries.permissiveRecovery`), which
      also disables the strict given even when a `Tactic[Parse.Error]` is present. It needs
      only a `Dom`. The parser recovers from a defined set of malformed inputs — unknown
      attributes, unclosed tags, stray close tags, tags in the wrong place — its warnings are
      discarded, and a condition it cannot recover from yields an empty `Html.Fragment`
      rather than an error.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
      import recoveries.permissiveRecovery

      t"<p>unclosed".read[Html of Flow] == P("unclosed")
      t"""<div bogus="x"></div>""".read[Html of "div"]
      ```
  entry .permissiveAggregable2   `read[Html]` under `recoveries.permissiveRecovery`: recovers, never raises
      As `Html.permissiveAggregable`, with every element of the `Dom` admitted at the top
      level. Needs only a `Dom`.
  entry .permissiveLoadable   `load[Html]` under `recoveries.permissiveRecovery`: recovers, never raises
      As `Html.strictLoadable`, but needing no `Tactic[Parse.Error]`: recoverable faults are
      repaired, a source with no single root is wrapped as it stands, and an unrecoverable one
      gives a document with an empty fragment as its root. Position tracking works as for the
      strict load.
  entry .streamable   `document.stream[Text]`: a `Document[Html]` as a chunked stream of its HTML source
      The `Streamable` given for `Document[Html]`. The doctype and the root are written
      through the document's own `Dom` (`metadata.dom`), laid out by the `Html.Formatting` in
      scope — indented by default, one line with `formatting.compactHtmlFormatting` — and
      produced on a separate fiber, so it needs a `Monitor` and a `Probate`: an ambient
      concurrency context such as `supervisors.globalSupervisor` with
      `threads.virtualThreads` and `probates.cancelProbate`. The serializer is the one
      `show` uses: void elements omit their close tag, `script` and `style` content is
      written verbatim, and text is escaped.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
      import probates.cancelProbate
      import supervisors.globalSupervisor
      import threads.virtualThreads

      val document = Document(Html(Head(Title("Hi")), Body(P("Hello"))), Html.Provenance(whatwg))
      document.stream[Text]
      ```
  entry .showable   `html.show`: any node or fragment as compact HTML source, against the WHATWG DOM
      The `Showable` given for every `Html` value, however refined its type. The rendering is
      one line with no indentation, through the standard WHATWG DOM whatever DOM the value was
      built in, so a bare node renders correctly — void elements unclosed, text escaped —
      outside any `Document`. `toString` of every node is its `show`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      Ol(Li("Alpha"), Li("Beta")).show == t"<ol><li>Alpha</li><li>Beta</li></ol>"
      Html.Comment("hello").show == t"<!--hello-->"
      Br.show == t"<br>"
      ```

      Text content escapes `&`, `<` and `>`; a double-quoted attribute value escapes `&` and
      `"`. The content of a raw-text element (`Html.Mode.Raw`: `script`, `style`) is written
      as it is.
  entry .inspectable   `html.inspect`: a node's compact source, escaped inside an `html"…"` literal
      The `Inspectable` given for every `Html` value: the same markup `show` produces, with
      its quotes and backslashes escaped, inside `html"…"`, so a `Div(style = t"bar")("hello")`
      inspects as `html"<div style=\"bar\">hello</div>"` — distinguishable from a `Text`
      holding the same markup, and from xylophone's `xml"…"`.
  entry .Vacuiscible   the mixin on an element made from a container tag: `apply(children*)` adds its children
    detail
        A `Tag.Container` applied to attributes alone, as `Div(style = t"bar")`, yields an
        `Html.Element & Html.Vacuiscible`, whose `apply` takes the children in a second
        argument list: `Div(style = t"bar")("hello")`. Each child must be `Html` of the
        element's `Transport` — what the tag admits — or `Unset`, which is dropped. The
        result is an element of the same topic and form.
    entry #apply   the element with the given children, each checked against what the tag admits
  entry .Transparent   the mixin on an element made from a transparent tag: its children decide its topic
    detail
        A transparent element — `<a>`, `<ins>`, `<del>`, `<map>`, `<object>` and the like —
        takes its content model from its parent, so `A(Li("x"))` cannot be placed in a `<div>`
        where `<li>` could not. `Html.Transparent#apply` records the children's labels as the
        result's `Topic`, so `A(Li("x"))` is an `Html.Element of "li"`, admitted
        exactly where an `<li>` is.
    entry #apply   the element with the given children, its topic becoming their labels
  entry .name   the format's name, `HTML`, as `Parse.Error` reports it
      `Html` is a `zephyrine.Format`, so a parse failure reads _the HTML was not valid at
      line 1, column 23 because the content ended while the tag `<ul>` was left open_.
  entry .text   converts a `Text` to an `Html of "#text"`, wherever text content is admitted
      The `Conversion` which lets `P(t"Hello")` and `h"<p>$name</p>"` take a `Text` where a
      child of a phrasing-content element is expected: the result is an `Html.Text` whose
      topic `"#text"` is a member of `Phrasing`. A `<ul>`, whose content excludes `"#text"`,
      rejects it.
  entry .string   converts a `String` to an `Html of "#text"`, wherever text content is admitted
      As `Html.text`, for string literals: `P("Hello world")`.
  entry .conversion3   widens an `Html of label` to an `Html of content` when `content` includes the label
      The `Conversion` which lets an `Html of "li"` stand where an `Html of "li" | "#text"`
      or an `Html of Flow` is expected: the label must be a member of the wider union.
  entry .comment   converts an `Html.Comment` to HTML of any content type
      A comment may appear anywhere, so it converts to `Html of content` for every content
      type.
  entry .string2   converts a `String` to foreign text, `Html of "#foreign"`
      Character data inside foreign content (SVG, MathML), as `Html.Element.foreign` builds
      it: `Html.Element.foreign(t"mi", attributes, "π")`.
  entry .renderable   converts any value `Renderable in content` to the HTML it renders
      With a `List[Text] is Renderable in "li"` in scope, a `List[Text]` converts to an
      `Html of "li"` wherever one is admitted — `Ul(items)`, `h"<ol>$items</ol>"` — and not
      where it is not.
  entry .sequences   lifts a conversion to HTML over a `List`: `List[nodal]` to `List[html]`
      Given a `Conversion[nodal, html]`, converts each element of a `List[nodal]`, so a list
      of text or of renderable values is a list of HTML where one is expected.
  entry .Issue   what the parser found wrong: the reason inside a `Parse.Error` from HTML
    detail
        An enum of `zephyrine.Format.Issue`s, each with a `describe` message, carried in the
        `Parse.Error(Html, position, issue)` a strict read raises, with an `Html.Position`.
        The cases: `BadInsertion` (_a value cannot be inserted into HTML at this point_, an
        `h"…"` substitution in a position no value fits); `ExpectedMore` (_the content ended
        prematurely_); `UnexpectedDoctype`; `BadDocument` (_the document did not contain a
        single root tag_, from `load[Html]`); `InvalidCdata` (_CDATA content is only
        permitted in foreign namespaces_); `InvalidTag(name)` (_`<name>` is not a valid tag_);
        `InvalidTagStart(prefix)`; `DuplicateAttribute(name)`; `InadmissibleTag(name,
        parent)` (_`<name>` cannot be a child of `<parent>`_); `OnlyWhitespace(char)` (a
        character where a whitespace-mode element admits only whitespace); `Unexpected(char)`;
        `UnknownEntity(name)` (_the entity `&name` is not defined_); `ForbiddenUnquoted(char)`
        (a character forbidden in an unquoted attribute value); `MismatchedTag(open, close)`;
        `UnopenedTag(close)`; `Incomplete(tag)` (_the content ended while the tag `<tag>` was
        left open_); `UnknownAttribute(name)`; `UnknownAttributeStart(name)`; and
        `InvalidAttributeUse(attribute, element)` (_the attribute `name` cannot be used on the
        tag `<tag>`_).

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import htmlDoms.whatwg
        import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        try
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          t"<ul><li>First item</li>".read[Html of Flow]
        catch
          case Parse.Error(Html, Html.Position(line, column, _, _), Html.Issue.Incomplete(tag)) =>
            tag == t"ul"
        ```

        An unknown attribute is `UnknownAttribute` when the parser read a whole name and
        `UnknownAttributeStart` when no attribute begins that way; a match should handle both.
        Under `recoveries.permissiveRecovery` the recoverable issues are repaired silently.
    entry #describe   the issue as a `Message`
  entry .Position   where in the source the parser was: line and column, with an offset and length
    detail
        The `zephyrine.Format.Position` of an HTML parse: `line` and `column` are `Ordinal`s
        (so `line.n1` is the 1-based number), and `offset` and `length` — present on an error
        with a known extent, and on every position `document.locate` returns — count chars
        from the start of the source. Lines and columns count code points, so a non-ASCII
        character is one column; lengths count chars. The column reported with an error is
        that of the next character to be read.
    entry #line   the line, as an `Ordinal`
    entry #column   the column on the line, as an `Ordinal`, counting code points
    entry #offset   the char offset from the start of the source, if known
    entry #length   the extent in chars, if known
    entry #describe   `line 2, column 8`, as `Parse.Error` reports it
    entry #span   the position as a line-mode `Span`
  entry .PositionIndex   the record of source positions a tracked `load[Html]` keeps on the document
      An opaque type over a frozen array of descriptors, one per element and attribute, built
      after the parse by walking the finished tree — HTML tree construction reorders nodes by
      inferring ancestors, fostering misplaced table content and reconstructing formatting
      elements, so positions cannot be emitted in source order. It is produced only under
      `parsing.trackPositions`, held in `Html.Provenance#positionIndex`, and read by
      `document.locate(path)` through `Html.positionable`; it has no public members of its
      own. An element the parser inferred starts at the token which prompted it and carries
      no attribute positions.
  entry .Provenance   a `Document[Html]`'s metadata: the DOM it was parsed against, and its position index
    detail
        The `Metadata` type of every `Html` value, so `Document(root, Html.Provenance(dom))`
        makes a document and `document.metadata.dom` is the `Dom` the streamed output is
        serialized through. `positionIndex` is set only by a `load[Html]` under
        `parsing.trackPositions`. The index is parse provenance, not identity: it takes no
        part in equality or hashing, so a tracked and an untracked load of the same source
        compare equal.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import htmlDoms.whatwg
        import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

        val document = Document(Html(Body(P("Hi"))), Html.Provenance(whatwg))
        document.metadata.positionIndex == Unset
        ```

        It is named `Provenance` rather than `Metadata` because the object `Html` is itself an
        element, whose `Metadata` type member a nested class of that name would clash with.
    entry #dom   the `Dom` the document was parsed against, and is serialized through
    entry #positionIndex   the `Html.PositionIndex`, or `Unset` for a document loaded without tracking
  entry .Path   a root-first path to an element or attribute of a document, for `document.locate`
    detail
        A path is built by appending steps to `Html.Path()`: `element(label, ordinal)` names
        a child element by its tag and its 1-based rank among the parent's child elements of
        that label — text and comments are not counted — and `attribute(name)` names an
        attribute of the last element. The first step names the document's root element
        itself, so a path into an ordinary document begins `element(t"html")`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import htmlDoms.whatwg
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import parsing.trackPositions

        val source = t"<html>\n  <body>\n    <p class=\"x\">Hi</p>\n  </body>\n</html>"
        val document = source.load[Html]
        val paragraph = Html.Path().element(t"html").element(t"body").element(t"p")
        document.locate(paragraph).let(_.line.n1)                        // 3
        document.locate(paragraph.attribute(t"class")).let(_.column.n1)  // 8
        document.locate(paragraph.element(t"missing"))                   // Unset
        ```

        A position spans the whole element, from its start tag to the end of its end tag; an
        element closed by its parent's end tag ends there. A path which does not resolve —
        a wrong root label, a missing child, an attribute the parser did not read — gives
        `Unset`, as does any lookup on a document loaded without `parsing.trackPositions`.
    entry .Step   one step of a path: a child element by label and rank, or an attribute by name
        An enum: `Element(label, rank)`, the `rank`-th (1-based) child element with that label,
        and `Attribute(name)`, which may only be the last step.
    entry #steps   the steps, root first
    entry #element   the path extended by a child element: `element(t"p")`, or `element(t"p", 2)` for the second
    entry #attribute   the path extended by an attribute of its last element
  entry .positionable   `document.locate(path)`: resolves an `Html.Path` to the `Html.Position` a tracked load recorded
      The `Positionable` given for `Document[Html]`, in implicit scope. `locate` walks the
      document's `Html.PositionIndex`; `locateKey` is always `Unset`, since HTML has no
      distinct key positions. Both are `Unset` for a document loaded without
      `parsing.trackPositions`.
  entry .Mode   how an element's content is tokenized and laid out: raw, RCDATA, whitespace or normal
      Every `Tag` carries a `Mode`, which the parser and the serializer look up in the DOM by
      name. `Raw` content (`script`, `style`) has no tags and no entities, and is written
      verbatim; `Rcdata` content (`textarea`, `title`) has entities but no tags; a
      `Whitespace` element (`html`, `head`, `ul`, `table`, …) admits only whitespace as text
      between its children — anything else is `Html.Issue.OnlyWhitespace` — and is laid out
      one child per indented line when a document streams under
      `formatting.indentedHtmlFormatting`; `Normal` is everything else.
  entry .Recovery   the marker whose presence in scope selects the recovering parser
    detail
        `Html.Recovery.Permissive` is a class with no members: a given of it in scope
        switches `read[Html]`, `read[Html of …]` and `load[Html]` from the strict givens,
        which need a `Tactic[Parse.Error]` and raise every `Html.Issue`, to the permissive
        ones, which need none and repair what they can. The given to import is
        `recoveries.permissiveRecovery`; it wins even when a tactic is also in scope, since
        the strict givens require that no `Permissive` be present.
    entry .Permissive   the marker class; `recoveries.permissiveRecovery` is its given
  entry .Hole   the kinds of position an `h"…"` substitution can occupy
      An enum the `h` interpolator's macro uses to type each substitution by where it falls:
      `Text` (character data), `Tagbody` (the attributes of a start tag, `h"<img $attributes>"`),
      `Comment` (`h"<!--$text-->"`), `Element(tag)` (a whole element, `h"<$element>"`),
      `Attribute(tag, attribute)` (an attribute's value, `h"<img src=$url>"`) and
      `Node(parent)` (child content of the named parent). It is not needed in ordinary code.
  entry .Node   exactly one node: an element, text, a comment or a doctype, but never a fragment
    detail
        The sealed trait of the single-node kinds — `Html.Element`, `Html.Text`,
        `Html.Comment` and `Html.Doctype` — as opposed to `Html.Fragment`, a sequence. An
        `Html` value is one or the other, so a match over both is exhaustive. `Html.Node`
        marks the few places which genuinely need one node, such as the children array an
        element holds and the result of `h"<$element>"`; where a sequence is as good, the
        type is `Html`. Nodes are pure, immutable values.
    entry #body   the node's children as a fragment: empty for a text, comment or doctype
  entry .Comment   a comment node, `<!--text-->`
    detail
        `Html.Comment(t"note")` renders as `<!--note-->`, and reads back from a comment in
        the source with its text as written, including surrounding spaces. A comment is
        admitted in any content, through `Html.comment`, and equals a fragment holding only
        it.
    entry #text   the comment's text, between the delimiters
  entry .Text   a text node: character data, with topic `"#text"`
    detail
        `Html.Text(t"Hello")` is what a `Text` or a string literal becomes as a child, through
        `Html.text` and `Html.string`, and what the parser yields for character data, with
        entities decoded: `t"to &amp; fro".read[Html of Flow] == Html.Text("to & fro")`. Its
        topic is `"#text"`, a member of `Phrasing`, so text is admitted exactly where the DOM
        admits it. It renders escaped (`&`, `<`, `>`), except inside a raw-text element; it
        equals a fragment holding only it. In a pattern, `case Html.Text("one")` matches the
        text of a captured body.

        Inside a scope which imports `Html.*`, the bare name `Text` means this node rather
        than `anticipation.Text`; write `Html.Text` instead.
    entry .foreign   character data inside foreign content, as `Html of "#foreign"`
        `Html.Text.foreign(t"π")` is a text node admitted inside an `Html.Element.foreign`
        (SVG, MathML), whose content is typed `"#foreign"`.
    entry #text   the character data
    entry #Topic   `"#text"`
  entry .Element   an element: its tag name, its attributes, its children and whether it is foreign
    detail
        `Html.Element(label, attributes, children, foreign)` is what every tag application
        builds — `Div(id = t"x")("hi")` is `Html.Element("div", Attributes(t"id" -> t"x"),
        Array(Html.Text("hi")), false)` — and what `case Html.Element(label, attributes,
        children, _)` takes apart. Its static type usually says more: `Html.Element of "img"
        in Whatwg`, or `Html.Element of "ul" over "li" in Whatwg`.

        A value statically known to be an element has three transformations, each giving a
        new element: `^+` adds a child at the start and `+^` at the end — the caret marks the
        end, and the child must be of the element's `Transport` — and an attribute is set,
        replaced or read by name, through `Dynamic`:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import htmlDoms.whatwg
        import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

        val image: Html.Element of "img" in Whatwg = Img(src = t"puppy.jpg")
        val described = image.alt = t"A young dog"       // <img src="puppy.jpg" alt="A young dog">
        described.alt                                    // t"A young dog"
        Div(P("body")) ^+ H1("title")                    // <div><h1>title</h1><p>body</p></div>
        Div(H1("title")) +^ (P("body") + P("more"))      // three children
        ```

        `/` selects child elements by tag: `table / Tbody / Tr / Th` is a fragment of every
        `<th>` reached that way, which, holding one node, equals that node. Equality is
        structural, with attributes compared in any order; an element equals a fragment of
        only itself. `toString` is the element's `show`.
    entry .foreign   an element of a foreign namespace — SVG, MathML — with its attributes and children
        `Html.Element.foreign(label, attributes, children*)` builds an element the DOM does
        not describe, typed `Html.Element of "#foreign"`, with `foreign = true`, from an
        `Attributes` or a `Map[Text, Optional[Text]]`. Its children are `Html of
        "#foreign"`: further foreign elements, or strings converted by `Html.string2`. The
        parser builds these for the content of `<svg>` and `<math>`:
        `t"<svg><circle r=\"1\"/></svg>".read[Html of Flow]` is
        `Svg(Html.Element.foreign(t"circle", Map[Text, Optional[Text]](t"r" -> t"1")))`.
    entry #label   the tag name, lowercase
    entry #attributes   the attributes, in order, as an `Attributes`
    entry #children   the child nodes, as a frozen `Array[Html.Node]`
    entry #foreign   whether the element is in a foreign namespace (SVG, MathML)
    entry #/   `element / tag`: the child elements with that tag, as a fragment typed by the tag
        `Div(Ul(Li("one"), Li("two"))) / Ul / Li` is `Html.Fragment(Li("one"), Li("two"))`:
        each step keeps the children whose label is the tag's and continues from them. The
        result is an `Html.Fragment of tag.Topic in tag.Form`, so the next step can only be
        a tag the previous admits.
    entry #body   the element's children as a fragment of its own topic, transport and form
    entry #^+   `element ^+ child`: the element with a child inserted first
        The child, a node or a fragment of the element's `Transport`, is placed before the
        existing children: `Div(P("body")) ^+ H1("title")` is `<div><h1>title</h1><p>body</p></div>`.
    entry #+^   `element +^ child`: the element with a child appended last
        The child, a node or a fragment of the element's `Transport`, is placed after the
        existing children: `Div(H1("title")) +^ P("body")`.
    entry #selectDynamic   `element.attribute`: reads an attribute, decoded by its `Unattributive`
        `image.alt` resolves the name as an `Attribute` of the element's topic in its DOM
        (or a global attribute), and decodes its text with the attribute type's
        `Unattributive`; a name which is not an attribute of that element, or whose type has
        no decoder, is a compile error.
    entry #updateDynamic   `element.attribute = value`: a new element with the attribute set, or removed
        `image.alt = t"…"` resolves the name as an `Attribute` of the element's topic in its
        DOM, encodes the value through a `value is Attributive to` the attribute's type, and
        gives a new element with the pair set, or with the attribute removed when the
        `Attributive` yields `Unset` (a `false` for a presence attribute, an empty class
        list). The original is unchanged. A `Text` satisfies every attribute type with
        `attributives.textAttributive` in scope.
  entry .Fragment   a sequence of nodes with no single parent
    detail
        `Html.Fragment(nodes*)` holds zero or more `Html.Node`s: the result of reading text
        with several top-level nodes, of `+`, of `/`, and of a `Renderable` which produces
        several elements. A fragment supplied as a child is unwrapped into its parent's
        children, and a fragment of one node equals that node, so `Html.Fragment(P("x")) ==
        P("x")`; an empty text reads as `Html.Fragment()`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import htmlDoms.whatwg
        import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

        val items: Html.Fragment of "li" = Html.Fragment(Li("one"), Li("two"))
        Ul(items) == Ul(Li("one"), Li("two"))
        ```
    entry .apply   `Html.Fragment[topic](nodes*)`: a fragment of the given nodes, typed by their topics
        Each argument is any `Html` whose topic is within `topic`; fragments among them are
        flattened. The companion's `apply` is what `Html.Fragment(Li("one"), Li("two"))`
        calls; the case-class constructor takes `Html.Node`s alone.
    entry #nodes   the nodes, in order
    entry #/   `fragment / tag`: the child elements with that tag of every node, concatenated
    entry #body   the fragment itself
  entry .Doctype   a document type declaration, `<!DOCTYPE text>`
    detail
        `Html.Doctype(t"html")` is the HTML5 header, which `Html.doctype` and
        `Whatwg#doctype` supply and a streamed `Document[Html]` writes first;
        `Html4Transitional#doctype` is the HTML 4.01 Transitional one. A doctype in a source
        is accepted by `load[Html]`, case-insensitively, and reported as
        `Html.Issue.UnexpectedDoctype` by `read[Html]`.
    entry #text   the declaration's text after `DOCTYPE`
  entry #Topic   the value's tag name, union of names or content category, as a literal type
      A `Label` subtype: `"br"` for a `<br>`, `"ul" | "ol"` for either list, `Flow` for any
      flow content, `"#text"` for a text node and `"#foreign"` for foreign content.
  entry #Transport   what the value may contain, as a union of tag names
      `"li"` for a `<ul>`, `Phrasing` for a `<p>`, `"head" | "body"` for `<html>`.
  entry #Form   the `Dom` the value conforms to: `Whatwg` or `Html4Transitional`
  entry #Metadata   `Html.Provenance`, what a `Document[Html]` carries beside its root
  entry #Chunks   `Text`: a document of HTML streams as text
  entry #/   `html / tag`: the child elements with that tag, as a fragment typed by the tag
      On an element, the children with the tag's label; on a fragment, those of every node;
      on any other node, empty. The result is an `Html.Fragment of tag.Topic in tag.Form`.
  entry #body   the value's children as a fragment of its own topic, transport and form
      An element's children, a fragment itself, and an empty fragment for a text, comment or
      doctype.

entry .html   `value.html`: any `Renderable` value as the HTML it renders
    An extension on every type with a `Renderable` instance: `message.html` is an `Html of
    Phrasing` for a `Message`, `stackTrace.html` an `Html of Flow` for a `StackTrace`, and a
    `Math` or `Mathml` renders through its own instance. The result type is the instance's
    `Form`, so it is admitted exactly where that content is.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import htmlDoms.whatwg
    import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

    val message: Message = m"the file was not found"
    P(message.html)
    ```

entry .h   the `h"…"` interpolator: an HTML literal parsed as the code compiles, and a pattern
    `h"<ol><li>Alpha</li><li>Beta</li></ol>"` parses its markup against the WHATWG DOM while
    the code compiles, reports any `Html.Issue` at the literal, and instantiates the nodes
    directly at runtime, so there is no parsing cost and no loss of checking. The standard
    parsing rules apply, so optional closing tags may be omitted:
    `h"<ol><li>Alpha<li>Beta</ol>"` is the same value. The result is typed by its top-level
    tags, in `Whatwg`.

    A substitution is allowed wherever it fits the surrounding markup, and is checked in its
    position: body content must be HTML admitted by the enclosing element (or convertible to
    it, through `Html.text`, `Html.renderable` and the other conversions), an attribute value
    must satisfy the attribute's type through an `Attributive`, and a comment's content must
    be `Showable`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import htmlDoms.whatwg
    import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
    import attributives.textAttributive

    val content = t"important"
    val location = t"https://example.com/dog.jpg"

    h"<p>$content</p>"          // a body-content substitution
    h"<img src=$location>"      // an attribute-value substitution
    h"<!--$content-->"          // a comment substitution
    ```

    As a pattern, a literal matches an equal node, whether the scrutinee is that node or a
    fragment holding only it, and is checked as HTML against the DOM rather than as text, so
    a pattern written with closing tags matches input written without them. A hole binds
    what it stands for: `h"<li>$value</li>"` binds the body as `Html`, `h"<img src=$source>"`
    an attribute's value, `h"<img $attributes>"` the attribute map, and `h"<$element>"` any
    element but no other kind of node.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import htmlDoms.whatwg
    import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

    h"<li>two</li>" match
      case h"<li>one</li>" => 1
      case h"<li>$value</li>" => value match
        case Html.Text("two") => 2
        case _                => 0
      case _ => 0
    ```

    The interpolator is `transparent inline`, resolving `Html.interpolator` and
    `Html.extrapolator`, whose macros live in `honeycomb.internal`.

entry .Attributes   an element's attributes: an ordered, immutable map from name to optional value
  detail
      The attributes of an `Html.Element`, in document order, as an opaque type over a
      frozen array of interleaved keys and values rather than a `Map`. A value is
      `Optional[Text]`: a presence attribute such as `disabled` or `checked` has `Unset` as its
      value and renders as the bare name. A parse builds one as it reads a start tag, every
      tag application builds one from its presets and arguments, and every update (`updated`,
      `removed`, `++`) returns a new value. It is declared in `honeycomb.internal` and
      exported to the `honeycomb` package.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      val attributes: Attributes = Attributes(t"class" -> t"a", t"hidden" -> Unset)
      attributes(t"class")                 // t"a"
      attributes(t"hidden")                // Unset: present, with no value
      attributes.contains(t"hidden")       // true
      attributes.updated(t"id", t"x").to[List]
      Html.Element(t"div", attributes, Array(), false)
      ```

      Equality of two `Attributes` is order-insensitive (`equalsAttributes`), which is what
      `Html.Element`'s own equality uses; iteration (`keys`, `iterator`, `each`, `to[List]`,
      `toMap`) is in document order. It inspects as `html{class="a", hidden}`, as the
      attributes would appear in a start tag.
  refer honeycomb.Html

entry .htmlDoms   the HTML specifications a parse and a tag application use, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Which tags exist, which children and attributes each admits, how its content is
      tokenized and which named character references are defined all come from a `Dom`,
      and the choice is never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none in, and one is
      imported by name. The tag objects themselves are members of the DOM's companion, so
      the usual pair of imports is the given and then its vocabulary:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      Div(P("Hello"))
      ```

      `Whatwg` is the HTML Living Standard, what "HTML" means today, and suits almost every
      use; `Html4Transitional` is HTML 4.01 Transitional, for a program which must emit or
      read that dialect's presentational tags and attributes. Reading (`read[Html]`,
      `load[Html]`) needs a `Dom` in scope; rendering with `show` always uses the WHATWG one,
      and a streamed `Document[Html]` uses the DOM recorded in its `Html.Provenance`.
  refer honeycomb.Whatwg
  refer honeycomb.Html4Transitional
  entry .whatwg   the WHATWG HTML Living Standard, as a `Whatwg` instance
      `import htmlDoms.whatwg` brings the `Dom` into scope, and `import htmlDoms.whatwg.*`
      — or `import whatwg.*` after it — brings in the tag objects `A` to `Wbr` and the
      content categories `Flow`, `Phrasing`, `Metadata`, `Heading`, `Sectioning`, `Embedded`
      and `Interactive`.
  entry .html4Transitional   HTML 4.01 Transitional, as an `Html4Transitional` instance
      `import htmlDoms.html4Transitional` and then `import html4Transitional.*` for its tags,
      which include `Font`, `Center` and the other presentational elements the
      Living Standard dropped, and whose `doctype` is the HTML 4.01 Transitional one.

entry .formatting   how a streamed `Document[Html]` is laid out, each an `Html.Formatting` given to import by name
  detail
      The `Html.Formatting` in scope decides whether `document.stream[Text]` writes the
      elements whose DOM mode is `Html.Mode.Whitespace` — `html`, `head`, `body`, `ul`,
      `ol`, `table`, `tr` and the rest, where whitespace between children is insignificant —
      one per line, indented two spaces per level, or keeps the whole document on one line.
      `Html.Formatting.default` is indented, so nothing need be imported for that; the compact
      layout is chosen by importing `compactHtmlFormatting`, which, as a lexically-scoped
      given, outranks the companion's default. Neither affects `show`, which is always compact.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Indented
        code
            import soundness.*
            import htmlDoms.whatwg
            import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
            import probates.cancelProbate
            import supervisors.globalSupervisor
            import threads.virtualThreads
            import formatting.indentedHtmlFormatting

            val page = Html(Head(Title("Hi")), Body(Ul(Li("one"), Li("two"))))
            val document = Document(page, Html.Provenance(whatwg))

            document.stream[Text]
            // <!DOCTYPE html>
            // <html>
            //   <head>
            //     <title>Hi</title>
            //   </head>
            //   <body>
            //     <ul>
            //       <li>one</li>
            //       <li>two</li>
            //     </ul>
            //   </body>
            // </html>
      alternative Compact
        code
            import soundness.*
            import htmlDoms.whatwg
            import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
            import probates.cancelProbate
            import supervisors.globalSupervisor
            import threads.virtualThreads
            import formatting.compactHtmlFormatting

            val page = Html(Head(Title("Hi")), Body(Ul(Li("one"), Li("two"))))
            val document = Document(page, Html.Provenance(whatwg))

            document.stream[Text]
            // <!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>Hi</title></head><body><ul><li>one</li><li>two</li></ul></body></html>
      ##
      ```

      A `<p>` or any other element whose text is significant is never broken across lines,
      whatever the formatting, so indentation never changes what a browser shows. The
      package merges with the umbrella's `formatting`, beside the other formats' layouts.
  refer honeycomb.Html
  entry .indentedHtmlFormatting   whitespace-mode elements one per indented line; the same as the default
  entry .compactHtmlFormatting   the whole document on one line

entry .attributives   `Attributive` givens which widen what an attribute accepts, to import by name
  detail
      An attribute's value is typed: `width` takes a positive integer, `src` a URL, `disabled`
      a presence, so `Img(width = 64)` compiles and `Img(width = 1.5)` does not. The
      `Attributive` instances in `Attributive`'s companion cover the common types; where an
      attribute type has no instance for the value in hand, `textAttributive` makes a `Text`
      acceptable for every attribute, so `Img(src = t"image.jpg")` stands where a URL is
      expected. It is imported by name, since it deliberately bypasses the attribute's type.
  refer honeycomb.Attributive
  entry .textAttributive   a `Text` is acceptable as the value of any attribute, written as it is
      `[target] => Text is Attributive to target`: the text becomes the attribute's value
      unchanged, whatever the attribute's declared type. With it in scope, `src = t"x.jpg"`,
      `width = t"64"` and `onclick = t"…"` all compile; without it, each needs a value of the
      attribute's own type.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
      import attributives.textAttributive

      Img(src = t"image.jpg", width = t"64")
      ```

entry .stylesheets   what the class-name syntax `P.info(…)` may name, as a given to import by name
  detail
      Because `class` is a Scala keyword, a class is named as a method on the tag:
      `P.info("body text")` is `<p class="info">body text</p>` — one name per application,
      which `applyDynamic` resolves through an
      `Attribution of name`. `uncheckedClasses` supplies that evidence for any name; binding a
      cataclysm stylesheet instead makes the names it defines the only legitimate ones, so a
      mistyped class is a compile error, and a name the stylesheet declares as an id renders
      as `id="…"`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
      import stylesheets.uncheckedClasses

      P.info("body text")      // <p class="info">body text</p>
      ```
  refer honeycomb.Attribution
  entry .uncheckedClasses   any name used as `Tag.name(…)` is a CSS class, unchecked
      An `Attribution of name` for every label but `apply` (which is the plain `Tag(…)`
      call): the name populates the `class` attribute, appended after any class the tag's
      presets already carry.

entry .recoveries   the recovering HTML parser, as a given to import by name
  detail
      The strict parsers behind `read[Html]` and `load[Html]` need a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`
      and raise every `Html.Issue`. Bringing `permissiveRecovery` into scope selects the
      permissive givens (`Html.permissiveAggregable`, `Html.permissiveAggregable2`,
      `Html.permissiveLoadable`) instead, which need no tactic, repair a defined set of
      malformed inputs the way a browser would — an unknown attribute is kept, an unclosed
      tag closed, a stray close tag dropped, a misplaced tag retried at an enclosing level —
      and yield an empty fragment or document rather than fail on anything else.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
      import recoveries.permissiveRecovery

      t"<p>unclosed".read[Html of Flow] == P("unclosed")
      t"<html><body><p>a</b></p></body></html>".load[Html]
      ```

      The permissive given wins even when a tactic is also in scope, since the strict givens
      require that no `Html.Recovery.Permissive` be present. Position tracking
      (`parsing.trackPositions`) works under either parser.
  refer honeycomb.Html
  entry .permissiveRecovery   an `Html.Recovery.Permissive`: selects the recovering parser

entry .construables   construes a `text/html` body as a `Document[Html]`, as a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Construable` binds a media type to the type its content is read as, so that a
      body received with that `Content-Type` — an HTTP response, an OpenAPI media binding — is
      decoded deliberately rather than by default. `htmlConstruable` binds `text/html` to
      `Document[Html]`; it is imported by name, and the document is then parsed through the
      `Dom` and `Html.Loadable` given in scope.
  entry .htmlConstruable   `"text/html" is Construable to Document[Html]`

entry .internal   the macros behind `h"…"`, tag application and `ClassList`, and the home of `Attributes`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home.
      `Attributes` is exported from here to the `honeycomb` package.
  entry .classList   the macro behind `ClassList[name]()`: checks each label against CSS identifier rules
      A label which is not a CSS identifier (`ClassList["1bad"]()`), or a type argument not
      made of string literals, is a compile error at expansion.
  entry .extractor   the macro behind `h"…"` as a pattern
      Parses the pattern's parts against the WHATWG DOM, with a hole at each substitution,
      and splices an extractor which matches the scrutinee structurally and binds each hole.
  entry .interpolator   the macro behind `h"…"` as an expression
      Parses the parts against the WHATWG DOM, checks each substitution against the
      `Html.Hole` it falls in, and splices the construction of the nodes.
  entry .attributes   the macro behind a tag's named-argument application, `Img(src = …)`
      Resolves each `name = value` argument as an `Attribute` of the tag's topic in its DOM,
      finds a `value is Attributive to` the attribute's type, and splices the element with
      the resulting pairs; a name which is not an attribute of the tag, or a value with no
      `Attributive`, is a compile error.
  entry .Attributes   an element's attributes; see `honeycomb.Attributes`
    detail
        The opaque type over a frozen array of interleaved keys and optional values, with its
        constructors, givens and extensions. Its page under the `honeycomb` package has the
        full account.
    entry .empty   no attributes
    entry .inspectable   renders the attributes as `html{class="a", hidden}`
    entry .traversable   the pairs in document order, so `attributes.to[List]` needs no conversion
    entry .apply   `Attributes(pairs*)` builds from pairs; `attributes(key)` is the value, or `Unset`
        The constructor keeps the pairs' order; a pair's value is `Unset` for a presence
        attribute. The lookup gives `Unset` both for a key which is absent and for one present
        with no value; `contains` tells them apart.
    entry .from   builds from a `Map[Text, Optional[Text]]`, in the map's iteration order
    entry .size   the number of attributes
    entry .isEmpty   whether there are no attributes
    entry .nonEmpty   whether there is at least one attribute
    entry .nil   whether there are no attributes
    entry .contains   whether a key is present, with or without a value
    entry .keys   the keys, in document order, as an `Iterator`
    entry .iterator   the `(key, value)` pairs, in document order, as an `Iterator`
    entry .toMap   the attributes as a `Map[Text, Optional[Text]]` which still iterates in document order
    entry .each   applies an action to each `(key, value)` pair, in order
    entry .removed   the attributes without a key; the same value if the key is absent
    entry .-   `attributes - key`, the same as `removed`
    entry .--   the attributes without each of several keys
    entry .updated   the attributes with a key set to a value, in place if present, appended if not
        The value is `Optional`: `updated(t"disabled", Unset)` sets a presence attribute.
    entry .++   the attributes merged with another `Attributes` or a `Map`, the right side winning
        Keys of the left keep their positions, with the right's values where both have a key,
        and the right's new keys follow in its order.
    entry .equalsAttributes   whether two attribute sets hold the same pairs, in any order
    entry .hashAttributes   an order-independent hash, consistent with `equalsAttributes`
    entry .showAttributes   a debugging rendering, `Attributes(class -> a, hidden -> Unset)`, as a `String`

entry .Whatwg   the WHATWG HTML Living Standard: its tags as a `Dom` value, and its attributes as givens
  detail
      `Whatwg` is the description of HTML which honeycomb uses unless another is chosen. It is
      two things which share a name. The **object** is the attribute vocabulary: one marker
      type for every kind of value an attribute may take (`Url`, `PositiveInt`, `Presence`,
      `CssClassList`, …) and one `Attribute` given for every attribute of the specification,
      each typed by its name, the elements it is valid on, and the marker of its value
      (`"src" is Attribute on "img" | "video" | … of Url in Whatwg`). The **class** is the
      `Dom`: a `val` for every tag (`Div`, `P`, `Img`, `Table`, …), the content categories as
      type aliases (`#Flow`, `#Phrasing`, `#Metadata`, …), the `<!doctype html>` doctype, the
      named character references, and the `#elements` and `#attributes` tables the parser
      and serializer read.

      A program takes the `Whatwg` instance as the `Dom` given, `htmlDoms.whatwg`, and
      imports its tags from it:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
      import attributives.textAttributive

      Div(id = t"main")(H1("Title"), P("Hello ", Em("world")))
      Img(src = t"puppy.jpg", width = 320, alt = t"A puppy")
      ```

      Every tag is a `Tag` whose type records its name, what it may contain and that it belongs
      to this dialect: `Div` is a `Tag.Container of "div" over Flow in Whatwg`, `Br` a
      `Tag.Void of "br" in Whatwg`, `A` a `Tag.Transparent of "a" over "" in Whatwg`. The
      attribute givens live in the companion, so they are in implicit scope without an import:
      `Img(src = …)` finds `"src" is Attribute on (? >: "img") in Whatwg`, reads its `Topic`
      (`Url`) and then summons a `value is Attributive to Url` for the value supplied — which
      is why a `Text` is refused for `src` unless `attributives.textAttributive` is imported,
      and why `Img(width = 1.5)` does not compile. An attribute which is not valid on the
      element (`P(src = …)`) is a compile error, _the attribute `src` cannot be used on the
      element `<p>`_.

      Where the specification gives one attribute name different meanings on different
      elements, there is a given per meaning, distinguished by a numeric suffix (`.href`,
      `.href2`, `.href3`; `.type` to `.type6`; `.value` to `.value5`): the suffix is only a
      name, since resolution is by attribute name and element.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A table's header
        code
            import soundness.*
            import htmlDoms.whatwg
            import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
            import attributives.textAttributive

            val header = Thead(Tr(Th("Name"), Th("Score")))
            Table(header).show   // <table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Score</th></tr></thead></table>
      frame
        label A body grown from data
        code
            val scores = List(t"Ada" -> 3, t"Bo" -> 1)
            val body = Tbody(scores.map { (name, score) => Tr(Td(name), Td(score.show)) }*)
            Table(header, body).show
      frame
        label A caption and a class
        code
            import stylesheets.uncheckedClasses

            Table.results(Caption("Results"), header, body).show
            // <table class="results"><caption>Results</caption><thead>…</thead><tbody>…</tbody></table>
      ##
      ```

      Not every rule of the specification is a type: where a rule depends on more than nesting
      (an `<audio>` whose children depend on its `src`, a `<button>` which may not contain
      interactive content) the type is the more permissive side, so a construction the
      specification allows is never rejected. The vocabulary follows the current Living
      Standard, so it has `<search>`, `<selectedcontent>`, `commandfor`, `closedby`,
      `popover` and `headingoffset`, and omits everything HTML 4 deprecated; the
      `Html4Transitional` dialect has those.
  refer honeycomb.Dom
  refer honeycomb.Tag
  refer honeycomb.Attribute
  refer honeycomb.Attributive
  refer honeycomb.Html4Transitional
  entry .Attribute   `honeycomb.Attribute in Whatwg`: an attribute of this dialect
      The alias every given in the companion is typed with, so an attribute given reads as
      `"alt" is Attribute on "img" of Textual` and is an `honeycomb.Attribute` whose `Form`
      is `Whatwg`. An attribute of the `Html4Transitional` dialect is a different type, so
      the two vocabularies never mix.
  entry .attribute   makes the given for an attribute valid on named elements
      `attribute["src", "img" | "video", Url]()` builds a `"src" is Attribute on "img" |
      "video" of Url in Whatwg`: the name from the first type argument, the element set from
      the second (a union of string literals, reified to a `Set[Text]`), and the value marker
      from the third. The companion's own givens are all made this way; a program extending
      the vocabulary — a data attribute, or an attribute of a custom element — defines a
      given of its own with it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given dataRole: ("data-role" is Whatwg.Attribute on "div" | "span" of Whatwg.Textual) =
        Whatwg.attribute()
      ```
  entry .globalAttribute   makes the given for an attribute valid on every element
      `globalAttribute["title", Textual]()` builds a `"title" is Attribute of Textual in
      Whatwg` with no element set and `global` true, so it `targets` every element. The
      specification's global attributes (`id`, `class`, `style`, `title`, `lang`, `dir`,
      `hidden`, `tabindex`, `accesskey`, and the rest) are all defined with it.
  entry .AccessKeys   the value marker of `accesskey`: an ordered set of key labels
  entry .Affirmation   the value marker of `translate`: `yes` or `no`
  entry .Autocapitalization   the value marker of `autocapitalize`: `off`, `none`, `on`, `sentences`, `words` or `characters`
  entry .ButtonType   the value marker of a `<button>`'s `type`: `submit`, `reset` or `button`
  entry .Closedby   the value marker of a `<dialog>`'s `closedby`: `any`, `closerequest` or `none`
  entry .Color   the value marker of a CSS colour, as `<link color>` takes
  entry .Colorspace   the value marker of an `<input type="color">`'s `colorspace`
  entry .Command   the value marker of a `<button>`'s `command`: a built-in command name or a custom `--name`
  entry .ContentEditable   the value marker of `contenteditable`: `true`, `false`, `plaintext-only` or empty
  entry .Coords   the value marker of an `<area>`'s `coords`: a comma-separated list of numbers
  entry .Crossorigin   the value marker of `crossorigin`: `anonymous` or `use-credentials`
      The marker only; the `honeycomb.Crossorigin` enum has the two values, but has no
      `Attributive` of its own, so it is passed as `.show` with `attributives.textAttributive`
      in scope.
  entry .CustomElementName   the value marker of `is`: a custom element name, containing a hyphen
  entry .Css   the value marker of `style`: inline CSS declarations
      `Attributive.style` accepts a `Text` for it.
  entry .CssClassList   the value marker of `class`: space-separated CSS class names
      Accepts a `Name[CssClass]`, a `List[Name[CssClass]]` or a `ClassList`
      (`Attributive.cssClass`, `.cssClassList` and `.classList`); an empty list omits the
      attribute altogether.
  entry .Datetime   the value marker of a `<del>` or `<ins>`'s `datetime`: a date, or a date and time
  entry .Decimal   the value marker of a floating-point attribute: `step`, `min`, `max` and `value` of `<meter>`, `high`, `low`, `optimum`
      `Attributive.double` accepts a `Double` for it.
  entry .Decoding   the value marker of an `<img>`'s `decoding`: `sync`, `async` or `auto`
  entry .Dir   the value marker of `dir`: `ltr`, `rtl` or `auto`
      `HDir` is the enum of its values, and carries its own `Attributive`, so
      `Body(dir = HDir.Rtl)` compiles with no further import.
  entry .Enctype   the value marker of `enctype` and `formenctype`: a form-encoding MIME type
  entry .EnterKeyHint   the value marker of `enterkeyhint`: `enter`, `done`, `go`, `next`, `previous`, `search` or `send`
  entry .FetchPriority   the value marker of `fetchpriority`: `high`, `low` or `auto`
  entry .HashName   the value marker of `usemap`: a `#`-prefixed reference to a `<map>`'s name
  entry .Hidden   the value marker of `hidden`: present, or `until-found`
  entry .HttpEquiv   the value marker of a `<meta>`'s `http-equiv`: a pragma directive name
      The `honeycomb.HttpEquiv` enum has the four directives, passed as `.show`.
  entry .Id   the value marker of `id`, and of attributes which name one element by its id
      `for`, `form`, `list`, `commandfor` and `popovertarget` all take an `Id`.
      `Attributive.domId` accepts a `Name[DomId]` for it.
  entry .Ids   the value marker of a space-separated list of ids: `headers`, `itemref`, an `<output>`'s `for`
      `Attributive.domIds` accepts a `List[Name[DomId]]`, omitting the attribute when the
      list is empty.
  entry .ImageSizes   the value marker of a `<link>`'s `imagesizes`: a source-size list for a preloaded image
  entry .ImageSrcSet   the value marker of a `<link>`'s `imagesrcset`: a srcset for a preloaded image
  entry .InputMode   the value marker of `inputmode`: `none`, `text`, `tel`, `url`, `email`, `numeric`, `decimal` or `search`
  entry .InputType   the value marker of an `<input>`'s `type`
      The `#Input` object offers each type as a preset tag (`Input.Email`, `Input.Checkbox`,
      …) so the attribute is rarely written directly.
  entry .InputValue   the value marker of an `<input>`'s `value`, whose form depends on the input's type
  entry .Integral   the value marker of a signed integer attribute: `tabindex`, `start`, an `<li>`'s `value`
      `Attributive.int` accepts an `Int` for it.
  entry .ItemProp   the value marker of `itemprop`: microdata property names
  entry .Kind   the value marker of a `<track>`'s `kind`: `subtitles`, `captions`, `descriptions`, `chapters` or `metadata`
      The `#Track` object offers each kind as a preset tag; the `honeycomb.Kind` enum has the
      values.
  entry .Language   the value marker of `lang`, `hreflang` and `srclang`: a BCP 47 language tag
  entry .Laziness   the value marker of `loading`: `lazy` or `eager`
  entry .MediaQueryList   the value marker of `media`: a CSS media query list
  entry .Method   the value marker of `method` and `formmethod`: `get`, `post` or `dialog`
      The `honeycomb.Method` enum has the three values, passed as `.show`.
  entry .Minmax   the value marker of an `<input>`'s `min` and `max`, whose form depends on the input's type
  entry .MimeList   the value marker of `accept`: a comma-separated list of MIME types and extensions
  entry .Mime   the value marker of a single MIME type: `type` on `<a>`, `<link>`, `<embed>`, `<object>` and `<source>`
  entry .Name   the value marker of `name`: a form-control, form, map, meta or slot name
  entry .OlType   the value marker of an `<ol>`'s `type`: `1`, `a`, `A`, `i` or `I`
  entry .Openness   the value marker of a `<template>`'s `shadowrootmode`: `open` or `closed`
  entry .PermissionsPolicy   the value marker of an `<iframe>`'s `allow`: a permissions policy
  entry .Popover   the value marker of `popover`: `auto`, `manual` or `hint`
  entry .PopoverAction   the value marker of `popovertargetaction`: `toggle`, `show` or `hide`
  entry .PositiveInt   the value marker of a non-negative integer attribute: `width`, `height`, `rows`, `cols`, `colspan`, `span`, `size`, …
      `Attributive.posInt` accepts an `Int` for it; the value is not checked for sign.
  entry .Presence   the value marker of a boolean attribute: present or absent, with no value
      `disabled`, `checked`, `hidden`'s plain form, `required`, `autofocus`, `controls`,
      `open`, and every other attribute whose presence is its meaning. `Attributive.boolean`
      accepts a `Boolean`: `true` writes the bare attribute (`<input disabled>`) and `false`
      omits it.
  entry .Preload   the value marker of `preload`: `none`, `metadata` or `auto`
      The `honeycomb.Preload` enum has the values, passed as `.show`.
  entry .ReferrerPolicy   the value marker of `referrerpolicy`: a referrer policy name
  entry .Regex   the value marker of an `<input>`'s `pattern`: a JavaScript regular expression
  entry .Sandbox   the value marker of an `<iframe>`'s `sandbox`: space-separated `allow-…` tokens
      The `honeycomb.Sandbox` enum has the tokens, passed as `.show`.
  entry .ScriptType   the value marker of a `<script>`'s `type`: `module`, `importmap`, a MIME type, or a data block type
  entry .Scripting   the value marker of an event-handler attribute, which takes a script rather than text
      No `Attributive` for `Text` exists for it, deliberately: a handler is supplied as a
      typed scripting value, such as a querencia foreign DOM expression, for which the
      scripting library provides the `Attributive to Whatwg.Scripting`. The WHATWG vocabulary
      declares no `on…` attribute givens itself; `Html4Transitional` does.
  entry .Shape   the value marker of an `<area>`'s `shape`: `circle`, `default`, `poly` or `rect`
  entry .Sizes   the value marker of a `<link rel="icon">`'s `sizes`: `any` or `WxH` tokens
  entry .Softness   the value marker of a `<textarea>`'s `wrap`: `soft` or `hard`
      The `honeycomb.Wrap` enum has the two values, passed as `.show`.
  entry .SourceSizeList   the value marker of an `<img>` or `<source>`'s `sizes`
  entry .Srcdoc   the value marker of an `<iframe>`'s `srcdoc`: a whole HTML document, as text
  entry .SrcSet   the value marker of `srcset`: candidate image sources with their descriptors
  entry .Switch   the value marker of `autocomplete`: `on` or `off`
      `Attributive.switch` accepts a `Boolean`, written as `on` or `off`; the
      `honeycomb.Autocomplete` enum has the same two values.
  entry .Target   the value marker of `target` and `formtarget`: a browsing-context name or keyword
      The `honeycomb.Target` enum has `_self`, `_blank`, `_parent`, `_top` and a named
      context, passed as `.show`.
  entry .Temporal   the value marker of a `<time>`'s `datetime`: any of the specification's date, time and duration forms
  entry .Textual   the value marker of an attribute which takes any text
      `alt`, `title`, `placeholder`, `content`, `label`, `slot`, `nonce`, `integrity`, and
      the rest. `Attributive.textTextual` and `.stringTextual` accept a `Text` or a `String`
      for it, without any import; the `attributives.textAttributive` choice given extends the
      same acceptance to every other marker.
  entry .ThScope   the value marker of a `<th>`'s `scope`: `row`, `col`, `rowgroup` or `colgroup`
  entry .Tokens   the value marker of a space-separated token list: `rel`, `blocking`
      The `honeycomb.Rel` enum has the link types, passed as `.show`.
  entry .Truth   the value marker of an attribute written `true` or `false`: `draggable`, `spellcheck`, `writingsuggestions`
      `Attributive.truth` accepts a `Boolean`, written as the word `true` or `false`, unlike a
      `Presence` attribute which is written or omitted.
  entry .Upto8   the value marker of `headingoffset`: an integer from 0 to 8
  entry .Url   the value marker of a URL-valued attribute: `href`, `src`, `action`, `cite`, `poster`, `data`, `itemid`, …
      `Attributive.url` accepts any value which is `Abstractable across Urls to Text` — an
      `HttpUrl`, or any typed URL — and a `Text` once `attributives.textAttributive` is in
      scope.
  entry .Urls   the value marker of a space-separated list of URLs: `ping`, `itemtype`
  entry .Utf8   the value marker of `charset` and `accept-charset`, whose only valid value is `utf-8`
  entry .abbr   `abbr` on `<th>`: an abbreviated header, as `Textual`
  entry .accept   `accept` on `<input>`: the file types accepted, as `MimeList`
  entry .acceptCharset   the form's accepted encoding, on `<form>`, as `Utf8`
      The given is registered under the name `acceptCharset` rather than the
      specification's `accept-charset`, so `Form(acceptCharset = …)` is how it is written and
      `accept-charset` is what the parser would need to see; the HTML 4 dialect spells it
      correctly.
  entry .accesskey   `accesskey` on every element: keyboard shortcut labels, as `AccessKeys`
  entry .action   `action` on `<form>`: the URL the form submits to, as `Url`
  entry .allow   `allow` on `<iframe>`: the frame's permissions policy, as `PermissionsPolicy`
  entry .allowfullscreen   `allowfullscreen` on `<iframe>`: a `Presence`
  entry .alpha   `alpha` on `<input>`: whether a colour input has an alpha channel, as `Presence`
  entry .alt   `alt` on `<area>`, `<img>` and `<input>`: replacement text, as `Textual`
  entry .as   `as` on `<link>`: the destination type of a preload, as `Textual`
  entry .async   `async` on `<script>`: a `Presence`
  entry .autocapitalize   `autocapitalize` on every element, as `Autocapitalization`
  entry .autocomplete   `autocomplete` on `<form>`: `on` or `off`, as `Switch`
  entry .autocomplete2   `autocomplete` on `<input>`, `<select>` and `<textarea>`, as `Switch`
  entry .autocorrect   `autocorrect` on every element, as `Textual`
  entry .autofocus   `autofocus` on every element: a `Presence`
  entry .autoplay   `autoplay` on `<audio>` and `<video>`: a `Presence`
  entry .blocking   `blocking` on `<link>`, `<script>` and `<style>`: render-blocking tokens, as `Tokens`
  entry .charset   `charset` on `<meta>`: the document encoding, as `Utf8`
  entry .checked   `checked` on `<input>`: a `Presence`
  entry .cite   `cite` on `<blockquote>`, `<del>`, `<ins>` and `<q>`: the source URL, as `Url`
  entry .class   `class` on every element: CSS class names, as `CssClassList`
      Written as `` `class` = … `` since `class` is a keyword; the usual way is the
      `Tag.name(children)` form (`Div.panel(…)`), which an `Attribution` in scope permits.
  entry .closedby   `closedby` on `<dialog>`, as `Closedby`
  entry .color   `color` on `<link>`: a mask icon's colour, as `Color`
  entry .colorspace   `colorspace` on `<input>`, as `Colorspace`
  entry .cols   `cols` on `<textarea>`: the visible width in characters, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .colspan   `colspan` on `<td>` and `<th>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .command   `command` on `<button>`: the command to invoke, as `Command`
  entry .commandfor   `commandfor` on `<button>`: the id of the target element, as `Id`
  entry .content   `content` on `<meta>`: the metadata value, as `Textual`
  entry .contenteditable   `contenteditable` on every element, as `ContentEditable`
  entry .controls   `controls` on `<audio>` and `<video>`: a `Presence`
  entry .coords   `coords` on `<area>`, as `Coords`
  entry .crossorigin   `crossorigin` on `<audio>`, `<img>`, `<link>`, `<script>` and `<video>`, as `Crossorigin`
  entry .data   `data` on `<object>`: the resource URL, as `Url`
  entry .datetime   `datetime` on `<del>` and `<ins>`: when the change was made, as `Datetime`
  entry .datetime2   `datetime` on `<time>`: the machine-readable form, as `Temporal`
  entry .decoding   `decoding` on `<img>`, as `Decoding`
  entry .default   `default` on `<track>`: a `Presence`
  entry .defer   `defer` on `<script>`: a `Presence`
  entry .dir   `dir` on every element: text direction, as `Dir`
      `Body(dir = HDir.Rtl)` compiles without an import, through `HDir.attributive`.
  entry .dirname   `dirname` on `<input>` and `<textarea>`: the field name for the direction, as `Textual`
  entry .disabled   `disabled` on `<button>`, `<input>`, `<optgroup>`, `<option>`, `<select>` and `<textarea>`: a `Presence`
  entry .disabled2   `disabled` on `<fieldset>`: a `Presence`
  entry .disabled3   `disabled` on `<link>`: a `Presence`
  entry .download4   `download` on `<a>` and `<area>`: the suggested filename, as `Textual`
  entry .draggable   `draggable` on every element: `true` or `false`, as `Truth`
  entry .enctype   `enctype` on `<form>`, as `Enctype`
  entry .enterkeyhint   `enterkeyhint` on every element, as `EnterKeyHint`
  entry .fetchpriority   `fetchpriority` on `<img>`, `<link>` and `<script>`, as `FetchPriority`
  entry .for   `for` on `<label>`: the id of the labelled control, as `Id`
      Written as `` `for` = … `` since `for` is a keyword.
  entry .for2   `for` on `<output>`: the ids of the contributing controls, as `Ids`
  entry .form   `form` on a form control: the id of the form it belongs to, as `Id`
      On `<button>`, `<fieldset>`, `<input>`, `<object>`, `<output>`, `<select>` and
      `<textarea>`.
  entry .formaction   `formaction` on `<input>` and `<button>`, as `Url`
  entry .formenctype   `formenctype` on `<input>` and `<button>`, as `Enctype`
  entry .formmethod   `formmethod` on `<input>` and `<button>`, as `Method`
  entry .formnovalidate   `formnovalidate` on `<input>` and `<button>`: a `Presence`
  entry .formtarget   `formtarget` on `<input>` and `<button>`, as `Target`
  entry .headers   `headers` on `<td>` and `<th>`: the ids of the cell's header cells, as `Ids`
  entry .headingoffset   `headingoffset` on every element: 0 to 8, as `Upto8`
  entry .headingreset   `headingreset` on every element, as `Presence`
      The given is registered under the attribute name `headingoffset`, not `headingreset`,
      so `headingreset = true` writes a bare `headingoffset`.
  entry .height   `height` on `<canvas>`, `<embed>`, `<iframe>`, `<img>`, `<input>`, `<object>`, `<video>` and `<svg>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .hidden   `hidden` on every element: present, or `until-found`, as `Hidden`
      Its marker is `Hidden`, not `Presence`, so `hidden = true` needs a given from
      `Boolean` to `Hidden`, which the library does not provide; with
      `attributives.textAttributive` in scope, `hidden = t""` writes the bare attribute.
  entry .high   `high` on `<meter>`, as `Decimal`
  entry .href   `href` on `<a>` and `<area>`: the link target, as `Url`
  entry .href2   `href` on `<base>`: the document base URL, as `Url`
  entry .href3   `href` on `<link>`: the linked resource, as `Url`
  entry .hreflang   `hreflang` on `<a>` and `<link>`, as `Language`
  entry .httpEquiv   `http-equiv` on `<meta>`: a pragma directive, as `HttpEquiv`
      Written `httpEquiv = …`; the attribute serializes as `http-equiv`.
  entry .id   `id` on every element, as `Id`
      Takes a `Name[DomId]` through `Attributive.domId`, or a `Text` with
      `attributives.textAttributive`.
  entry .imagesizes   `imagesizes` on `<link>`, as `ImageSizes`
  entry .imagesrcset   `imagesrcset` on `<link>`, as `ImageSrcSet`
  entry .inert   `inert` on every element: a `Presence`
  entry .inputmode   `inputmode` on every element, as `InputMode`
  entry .integrity   `integrity` on `<link>` and `<script>`: a subresource integrity hash, as `Textual`
  entry .is   `is` on every element: the custom element name, as `CustomElementName`
  entry .ismap   `ismap` on `<img>`: a `Presence`
  entry .itemid   `itemid` on every element: a microdata item's global identifier, as `Url`
  entry .itemprop   `itemprop` on every element, as `ItemProp`
  entry .itemref   `itemref` on every element, as `Ids`
  entry .itemscope   `itemscope` on every element: a `Presence`
  entry .itemtype   `itemtype` on every element, as `Urls`
  entry .kind   `kind` on `<track>`, as `Kind`
  entry .label   `label` on `<optgroup>`, `<option>` and `<track>`: a user-visible label, as `Textual`
      A given named `label` is also exported by other Soundness libraries, so in the
      `soundness` umbrella the name is reached through `Whatwg.label` where a bare reference
      would be ambiguous; attribute resolution itself never needs the name.
  entry .lang   `lang` on every element, as `Language`
  entry .list   `list` on `<input>`: the id of a `<datalist>`, as `Id`
  entry .loading   `loading` on `<img>` and `<iframe>`: `lazy` or `eager`, as `Laziness`
  entry .loop   `loop` on `<audio>` and `<video>`: a `Presence`
  entry .low   `low` on `<meter>`, as `Decimal`
  entry .max   `max` on `<input>`, as `Minmax`
  entry .max2   `max` on `<meter>` and `<progress>`, as `Decimal`
  entry .maxlength   `maxlength` on `<input>` and `<textarea>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .media   `media` on `<a>`, `<area>`, `<link>`, `<source>` and `<style>`, as `MediaQueryList`
  entry .method   `method` on `<form>`: `get`, `post` or `dialog`, as `Method`
  entry .min   `min` on `<input>`, as `Minmax`
  entry .min2   `min` on `<meter>`, as `Decimal`
  entry .minlength   `minlength` on `<input>` and `<textarea>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .multiple   `multiple` on `<input>` and `<select>`: a `Presence`
  entry .muted   `muted` on `<audio>` and `<video>`: a `Presence`
  entry .name   `name` on `<button>`, `<fieldset>`, `<input>`, `<output>`, `<select>` and `<textarea>`, as `Name`
  entry .name2   `name` on `<details>`: the exclusive-accordion group, as `Name`
  entry .name3   `name` on `<form>`, as `Name`
  entry .name4   `name` on `<iframe>` and `<object>`: a browsing-context name, as `Target`
  entry .name5   `name` on `<map>`, as `Name`
  entry .name6   `name` on `<meta>`: the metadata name, as `Name`
      The `#Meta` object offers the standard names as preset tags (`Meta.Viewport`,
      `Meta.Description`, …).
  entry .name7   `name` on `<slot>`, as `Name`
  entry .nomodule   `nomodule` on `<script>`: a `Presence`
  entry .nonce   `nonce` on every element: a cryptographic nonce, as `Textual`
  entry .novalidate   `novalidate` on `<form>`: a `Presence`
  entry .open   `open` on `<details>` and `<dialog>`: a `Presence`
  entry .optimum   `optimum` on `<meter>`, as `Decimal`
  entry .pattern   `pattern` on `<input>`, as `Regex`
  entry .ping   `ping` on `<a>` and `<area>`: URLs to notify, as `Urls`
  entry .placeholder   `placeholder` on `<input>` and `<textarea>`, as `Textual`
  entry .playsinline   `playsinline` on `<video>`: a `Presence`
  entry .popover   `popover` on every element, as `Popover`
  entry .popovertarget   `popovertarget` on `<button>` and `<input>`: the popover's id, as `Id`
  entry .pta   `popovertargetaction` on `<button>` and `<input>`, as `PopoverAction`
  entry .poster   `poster` on `<video>`: the placeholder image, as `Url`
  entry .preload   `preload` on `<audio>` and `<video>`, as `Preload`
  entry .readonly   `readonly` on `<input>` and `<textarea>`: a `Presence`
  entry .referrerpolicy   `referrerpolicy` on `<a>`, `<area>`, `<iframe>`, `<img>`, `<link>` and `<script>`, as `ReferrerPolicy`
  entry .rel   `rel` on `<a>` and `<area>`: link types, as `Tokens`
  entry .rel2   `rel` on `<link>`: link types, as `Tokens`
      The `#Link` object offers each link type as a preset tag (`Link.Stylesheet`,
      `Link.Icon`, …).
  entry .required   `required` on `<input>`, `<select>` and `<textarea>`: a `Presence`
  entry .reversed   `reversed` on `<ol>`: a `Presence`
  entry .rows   `rows` on `<textarea>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .rowspan   `rowspan` on `<td>` and `<th>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .sandbox   `sandbox` on `<iframe>`, as `Sandbox`
  entry .scope   `scope` on `<th>`, as `ThScope`
  entry .selected   `selected` on `<option>`: a `Presence`
  entry .shadowrootclonable   `shadowrootclonable` on `<template>`: a `Presence`
  entry .srcer   `shadowrootcustomelementregistry` on `<template>`: a `Presence`
  entry .srdf   `shadowrootdelegatesfocus` on `<template>`: a `Presence`
  entry .shadowrootmode   `shadowrootmode` on `<template>`: `open` or `closed`, as `Openness`
  entry .shadowrootserializable   `shadowrootserializable` on `<template>`: a `Presence`
  entry .shape   `shape` on `<area>`, as `Shape`
  entry .size   `size` on `<input>` and `<select>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .sizes   `sizes` on `<img>` and `<source>`, as `SourceSizeList`
  entry .sizes2   `sizes` on `<link>`: icon sizes, as `Sizes`
  entry .slot   `slot` on every element: the shadow-tree slot name, as `Textual`
  entry .span   `span` on `<col>` and `<colgroup>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .spellcheck   `spellcheck` on every element: `true` or `false`, as `Truth`
  entry .src   `src` on `<audio>`, `<embed>`, `<iframe>`, `<img>`, `<input>`, `<script>`, `<source>`, `<track>` and `<video>`, as `Url`
  entry .srcdoc   `srcdoc` on `<iframe>`: an inline document, as `Srcdoc`
  entry .srclang   `srclang` on `<track>`, as `Language`
  entry .srcset   `srcset` on `<img>` and `<source>`, as `SrcSet`
  entry .start   `start` on `<ol>`: the first item's number, as `Integral`
  entry .step   `step` on `<input>`, as `Decimal`
  entry .style   `style` on every element: inline CSS, as `Css`
  entry .tabindex   `tabindex` on every element, as `Integral`
  entry .target   `target` on `<a>` and `<area>`, as `Target`
  entry .target2   `target` on `<base>`: the default browsing context, as `Target`
  entry .target3   `target` on `<form>`, as `Target`
  entry .title   `title` on every element: advisory text, as `Textual`
  entry .translate   `translate` on every element: `yes` or `no`, as `Affirmation`
  entry .type   `type` on `<a>` and `<link>`: the linked resource's MIME type, as `Mime`
      Written as `` `type` = … `` since `type` is a keyword.
  entry .type2   `type` on `<button>`: `submit`, `reset` or `button`, as `ButtonType`
  entry .type3   `type` on `<embed>`, `<object>` and `<source>`, as `Mime`
  entry .type4   `type` on `<input>`, as `InputType`
      Rarely written: `#Input` has a preset tag per type (`Input.Email`, `Input.Range`, …).
  entry .type5   `type` on `<ol>`: the numbering style, as `OlType`
  entry .type6   `type` on `<script>`, as `ScriptType`
  entry .usemap   the image-map reference on `<img>`, `<input>` and `<object>`, as `HashName`
      The given is registered under the attribute name `usemaptype`, not the specification's
      `usemap`, so what it writes is `usemaptype="#map"`.
  entry .value   `value` on `<button>` and `<option>`, as `Textual`
  entry .value2   `value` on `<data>`: the machine-readable value, as `Textual`
  entry .value3   `value` on `<input>`, as `InputValue`
  entry .value4   `value` on `<li>`: the item's ordinal, as `Integral`
  entry .value5   `value` on `<meter>` and `<progress>`, as `Decimal`
  entry .width   `width` on `<canvas>`, `<embed>`, `<iframe>`, `<img>`, `<input>`, `<object>`, `<video>` and `<svg>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .wrap   `wrap` on `<textarea>`: `soft` or `hard`, as `Softness`
  entry .writingsuggestions   `writingsuggestions` on every element: `true` or `false`, as `Truth`
  entry #doctype   the `<!doctype html>` doctype a `Document[Html]` of this dialect carries
  entry #Interactive   the interactive content category, as a union of tag names
      `<a>`, `<audio>`, `<button>`, `<details>`, `<embed>`, `<iframe>`, `<img>`, `<input>`,
      `<label>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>` and `<video>`. The categories are types, so an
      `Html of Interactive` is any interactive element.
  entry #Flow   the flow content category: everything that may go in a `<body>`, `<div>` or `<li>`
      Every heading, every phrasing element, the sectioning elements, and the block-level
      elements (`<address>`, `<blockquote>`, `<details>`, `<dialog>`, `<div>`, `<dl>`,
      `<fieldset>`, `<figure>`, `<footer>`, `<form>`, `<header>`, `<hr>`, `<main>`, `<menu>`,
      `<ol>`, `<p>`, `<pre>`, `<table>`, `<ul>`, `<search>`). `Html of Flow` is the type most
      code wants for a piece of page content, and `List[Html of Flow]` for several.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      val nodes: List[Html of Flow] = List(Hr, P("Hello"), Br)
      ```
  entry #Phrasing   the phrasing content category: the text of a document and the elements which mark it up
      Includes `"#text"`, so text may appear wherever phrasing content may, and every
      embedded and interactive-phrasing element besides the inline elements (`<abbr>`,
      `<b>`, `<code>`, `<em>`, `<span>`, `<time>`, …). A `<p>` or a heading is
      `over Phrasing`; a `Renderable in Phrasing` may be placed inside either.
  entry #Embedded   the embedded content category: `<audio>`, `<canvas>`, `<embed>`, `<iframe>`, `<img>`, `<object>`, `<picture>`, `<video>`, `<math>` and `<svg>`
  entry #Sectioning   the sectioning content category: `<article>`, `<aside>`, `<nav>` and `<section>`
  entry #ScriptSupporting   `<script>` and `<template>`, which may appear wherever a list or table's own children may
  entry #Metadata   the metadata content category: what a `<head>` may contain
      `<base>`, `<link>`, `<meta>`, `<noscript>`, `<script>`, `<style>`, `<template>` and
      `<title>`.
  entry #Heading   `<h1>` to `<h6>` and `<hgroup>`
  entry #insertable   the tags which the parser may insert unwritten between a tag and a child
      `insertable(tag)` is the subset of `tag`'s admissible children which are marked
      `insertable`: `<html>`'s `<head>` and `<body>`, `<table>`'s `<tbody>`, `<tbody>`'s
      `<tr>`, `<colgroup>`. Used by `#infer`.
  entry #infer   the tag to insert between a parent and a child the parent cannot hold directly
      `infer(parent, child)` is the insertable child of `parent` through which `child` can be
      reached, or `Unset`, which is how `<table><tr>` parses as `<table><tbody><tr>` and
      `<p>Hello` read as a document gains its `<html>` and `<body>`. The `Dom` contract;
      the parser calls it.
  entry #elements   every tag of the dialect, by its name
      A `Dictionary[Tag]` built from the `Tag` members of this instance; the parser looks a
      start tag up here, and `Dom#generic` lists its keys.
  entry #entities   the named character references of HTML, by name
      The HTML 4 set and the Living Standard's additions, name to replacement text
      (`amp` to `&`, `nbsp` to a no-break space, and over two thousand more). The parser
      reads it; an unknown reference (`&foo;`) is left literally in the text.
  entry #attributes   every attribute of the dialect, by its name
      A `Dictionary[Attribute]` built from the attribute givens of the companion, with the
      several givens for one name (`href`, `href2`, `href3`) merged into one `Attribute`
      whose element set is the union. The parser consults it to reject an unknown attribute
      on a known element (`Html.Issue.UnknownAttribute`).
  entry #Html   the `<html>` root element, as `honeycomb.Html`
      A lazy alias so that `import htmlDoms.whatwg.*` brings `Html` into scope as a tag
      alongside the others: `Html(Head(Title("x")), Body(P("y")))` is the whole document
      tree. The `Html` object itself is the companion of the `Html` type, and is a
      `Tag.Container of "html" over "head" | "body"`.
  entry #A   `<a>`: a hyperlink; transparent, so it holds whatever its parent may
      `Tag.Transparent of "a" over "" in Whatwg`. It takes `href`, `target`, `download`,
      `rel`, `hreflang`, `type`, `referrerpolicy` and `ping`. Being transparent, an
      `A(…)` inside a `<p>` may hold phrasing content and one inside a `<div>` flow content;
      the parser rejects a child the enclosing element could not hold
      (`Html.Issue.InadmissibleTag`).
  entry #Abbr   `<abbr>`: an abbreviation, over phrasing content; `title` gives the expansion
  entry #Address   `<address>`: contact information, over flow content less headings, sectioning and nested addresses
  entry #Area   `<area>`: a region of an image map; a void tag with preset shapes
    detail
        A `Tag.Void` whose `Topic` is `"area"`, taking `alt`, `coords`, `shape`, `href`,
        `target`, `download`, `ping`, `rel` and `referrerpolicy`. Its four members preset
        the `shape` attribute; note that in the current source they are built with the
        label `input`, so `Area.Rect` serializes as `<input shape="rect">`.
    entry .Default   a `shape="default"` area: the whole image
    entry .Rect   a `shape="rect"` area, with `coords` as left, top, right, bottom
    entry .Circle   a `shape="circle"` area, with `coords` as centre x, centre y, radius
    entry .Poly   a `shape="poly"` area, with `coords` as a list of vertex pairs
    entry .Topic   `"area"`
    entry .Transport   `""`: no children
    entry .Form   `Whatwg`
  entry #Article   `<article>`: a self-contained composition, over flow content
  entry #Aside   `<aside>`: tangential content, over flow content
  entry #Audio   `<audio>`: a sound; transparent, plus `<source>` and `<track>` children
      Takes `src`, `crossorigin`, `preload`, `autoplay`, `loop`, `muted` and `controls`.
      The specification's rule that its children depend on whether `src` is present is not a
      type: both forms compile.
  entry #B   `<b>`: text drawn to attention without added importance, over phrasing content
  entry #Base   `<base>`: the document base URL and default link target; void, in `<head>`
      Takes `href` and `target`; the specification requires at least one, which is not
      enforced.
  entry #Bdi   `<bdi>`: text isolated for bidirectional formatting, over phrasing content
  entry #Bdo   `<bdo>`: text with an explicit direction, over phrasing content; `dir` is required
  entry #Blockquote   `<blockquote>`: a quoted section, over flow content; `cite` gives its source
  entry #Body   `<body>`: the document's content, over flow content
      Autoclosing and insertable: the parser supplies it around content read as a whole
      document, and closes it at the end of input.
  entry #Br   `<br>`: a line break; void
  entry #Button   `<button>`: a button, over phrasing content
      A boundary tag: the parser does not let a formatting element opened outside it
      continue inside it. Takes `type`, `disabled`, `form`, `formaction`, `formenctype`,
      `formmethod`, `formnovalidate`, `formtarget`, `name`, `value`, `command`, `commandfor`,
      `popovertarget` and `popovertargetaction`.
  entry #Canvas   `<canvas>`: a scriptable bitmap; transparent, so it holds fallback content
      Takes `width` and `height`.
  entry #Caption   `<caption>`: a table's title, over flow content; first child of `<table>`
  entry #Cite   `<cite>`: the title of a work, over phrasing content
  entry #Code   `<code>`: a fragment of computer code, over phrasing content
  entry #Col   `<col>`: a table column; void, inside `<colgroup>`; `span` covers several
  entry #Colgroup   `<colgroup>`: a group of columns, over `<col>` elements
      Only whitespace may appear between its children (`Html.Mode.Whitespace`), and it is
      insertable, so a `<col>` met directly in a `<table>` gets one.
  entry #Data   `<data>`: content with a machine-readable `value`, over phrasing content
  entry #Datalist   `<datalist>`: predefined options for an `<input list=…>`, over phrasing content and `<option>`s
  entry #Dd   `<dd>`: a description in a `<dl>`, over flow content; autoclosing
  entry #Del   `<del>`: removed text; transparent, with `cite` and `datetime`
  entry #Details   `<details>`: a disclosure widget: a `<summary>` then flow content
      Takes `open` and `name`, the latter grouping several into an exclusive accordion.
  entry #Dfn   `<dfn>`: the defining instance of a term, over phrasing content
  entry #Dialog   `<dialog>`: a dialog box, over flow content; takes `open` and `closedby`
  entry #Div   `<div>`: a generic container, over flow content
  entry #Dl   `<dl>`: a description list, over `<dt>`, `<dd>` and `<div>` groups
      Only whitespace may appear between its children, and it autocloses.
  entry #Dt   `<dt>`: a term in a `<dl>`, over flow content; autoclosing
  entry #Em   `<em>`: stress emphasis, over phrasing content
  entry #Embed   `<embed>`: an external application or interactive content; void
      Takes `src`, `type`, `width` and `height`.
  entry #Fieldset   `<fieldset>`: a group of form controls: an optional `<legend>` then flow content
      Takes `disabled`, `form` and `name`.
  entry #Figcaption   `<figcaption>`: a figure's caption, over flow content
  entry #Figure   `<figure>`: self-contained content with an optional `<figcaption>`, over flow content
  entry #Footer   `<footer>`: a footer for its nearest ancestor section, over flow content
  entry #Form   `<form>`: a form, over flow content
      Takes `action`, `method`, `enctype`, `accept-charset` (as `acceptCharset`),
      `autocomplete`, `name`, `novalidate` and `target`.
  entry #H1   `<h1>`: a heading of the first rank, over phrasing content
  entry #H2   `<h2>`: a heading of the second rank, over phrasing content
  entry #H3   `<h3>`: a heading of the third rank, over phrasing content
  entry #H4   `<h4>`: a heading of the fourth rank, over phrasing content
  entry #H5   `<h5>`: a heading of the fifth rank, over phrasing content
  entry #H6   `<h6>`: a heading of the sixth rank, over phrasing content
  entry #Head   `<head>`: the document's metadata, over `#Metadata` content
      Only whitespace may appear between its children; it autocloses, and is insertable, so
      a `<title>` met at the start of a document gets a `<head>` around it.
  entry #Header   `<header>`: introductory content for its nearest ancestor section, over flow content; autoclosing
  entry #Hgroup   `<hgroup>`: a heading with its subheadings, over `<p>` and `<h1>` to `<h6>`
  entry #Hr   `<hr>`: a thematic break; void
  entry #I   `<i>`: text in an alternate voice, over phrasing content
  entry #Iframe   `<iframe>`: a nested browsing context; void in this vocabulary
      Takes `src`, `srcdoc`, `name`, `sandbox`, `allow`, `allowfullscreen`, `width`,
      `height`, `referrerpolicy` and `loading`. It is defined as a `Tag.Void`, so it takes
      no children and serializes as `<iframe …>` with no end tag, where the specification
      requires `</iframe>`.
  entry #Img   `<img>`: an image; void
      Takes `src`, `alt`, `srcset`, `sizes`, `crossorigin`, `usemap`, `ismap`, `width`,
      `height`, `referrerpolicy`, `decoding`, `loading` and `fetchpriority`. `alt` is read
      back with `img.alt` and `width` with `img.width` (an `Optional[Int]`), through
      `Unattributive`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
      import attributives.textAttributive

      val image: Html.Element of "img" in Whatwg = Img(src = t"puppy.jpg", width = 50)
      image.width                               // 50
      (image.alt = t"A puppy").show             // <img src="puppy.jpg" width="50" alt="A puppy">
      ```
  entry #Input   `<input>`: a form control; a void tag with a preset for every `type`
    detail
        A `Tag.Void` whose `Topic` is `"input"`. `Input` alone is an input with no `type`
        (a text field); each member presets `type`, so `Input.Email(name = t"email")` is
        `<input type="email" name="email">`, and the parser reads `<input type="button">`
        back as `Input.Button`. An input takes `name`, `value`, `disabled`, `readonly`,
        `required`, `checked`, `placeholder`, `autocomplete`, `list`, `min`, `max`, `step`,
        `pattern`, `size`, `maxlength`, `minlength`, `multiple`, `accept`, `alt`, `src`,
        `width`, `height`, `form`, `formaction` and its siblings, `dirname`, `popovertarget`
        and `popovertargetaction`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import htmlDoms.whatwg
        import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
        import attributives.textAttributive

        Form(action = t"/signup")
          ( Input.Email(name = t"email", required = true),
            Input.Password(name = t"password"),
            Input.Checkbox(name = t"terms", checked = true),
            Input.Submit(value = t"Sign up") )
        ```
    entry .Hidden   `<input type="hidden">`
    entry .Text   `<input type="text">`
    entry .Search   `<input type="search">`
    entry .Tel   `<input type="tel">`
    entry .Url   `<input type="url">`
    entry .Email   `<input type="email">`
    entry .Password   `<input type="password">`
    entry .Date   `<input type="date">`
    entry .Month   `<input type="month">`
    entry .Week   `<input type="week">`
    entry .Time   `<input type="time">`
    entry .DatetimeLocal   `<input type="datetime-local">`
    entry .Number   `<input type="number">`
    entry .Range   `<input type="range">`
    entry .Color   `<input type="color">`
    entry .Checkbox   `<input type="checkbox">`
    entry .Radio   `<input type="radio">`
    entry .Submit   `<input type="submit">`
    entry .Image   `<input type="image">`
    entry .Reset   `<input type="reset">`
    entry .Button   `<input type="button">`
    entry .Topic   `"input"`
    entry .Transport   `""`: no children
    entry .Form   `Whatwg`
  entry #Ins   `<ins>`: inserted text; transparent, with `cite` and `datetime`
  entry #Kbd   `<kbd>`: user input, over phrasing content
  entry #Label   `<label>`: a caption for a form control, over phrasing content; `for` names the control
  entry #Legend   `<legend>`: a `<fieldset>`'s caption, over phrasing content and headings
      In the current source the tag is built with the label `label`, so a `Legend(…)`
      serializes as `<label>`; the parser's `<legend>` resolves through `#elements` by that
      label too.
  entry #Li   `<li>`: a list item, over flow content; autoclosing; `value` sets its ordinal in an `<ol>`
  entry #Link   `<link>`: a link to an external resource; a void tag with a preset for every `rel`
    detail
        A `Tag.Void` whose `Topic` is `"link"`, taking `href`, `rel`, `type`, `media`,
        `hreflang`, `sizes`, `as`, `crossorigin`, `integrity`, `referrerpolicy`, `blocking`,
        `disabled`, `color`, `imagesizes`, `imagesrcset` and `fetchpriority`. Each member
        presets `rel`, so `Link.Stylesheet(href = t"/style.css")` is
        `<link rel="stylesheet" href="/style.css">`, and the parser reads
        `<link rel="stylesheet">` back as `Link.Stylesheet`.
    entry .Alternate   `<link rel="alternate">`
    entry .Canonical   `<link rel="canonical">`
    entry .Author   `<link rel="author">`
    entry .DnsPrefetch   `<link rel="dns-prefetch">`
    entry .Expect   `<link rel="expect">`
    entry .Help   `<link rel="help">`
    entry .Icon   `<link rel="icon">`
    entry .Manifest   `<link rel="manifest">`
    entry .Modulepreload   `<link rel="modulepreload">`
    entry .License   `<link rel="license">`
    entry .Next   `<link rel="next">`
    entry .Pingback   `<link rel="pingback">`
    entry .Preconnect   `<link rel="preconnect">`
    entry .Prefetch   `<link rel="prefetch">`
    entry .Preload   `<link rel="preload">`
    entry .Prev   `<link rel="prev">`
    entry .PrivacyPolicy   `<link rel="privacy-policy">`
    entry .Search   `<link rel="search">`
    entry .Stylesheet   `<link rel="stylesheet">`
    entry .TermsOfService   `<link rel="terms-of-service">`
    entry .Topic   `"link"`
    entry .Transport   `""`: no children
    entry .Form   `Whatwg`
  entry #Main   `<main>`: the dominant content of the document, over flow content
  entry #Map   `<map>`: an image map; transparent, plus `<area>` children; `name` is required
  entry #Mark   `<mark>`: highlighted text, over phrasing content
  entry #Math   `<math>`: MathML content, foreign to HTML
      A foreign tag: its children are not HTML elements but `Html.Element.foreign` nodes,
      which the parser builds for any tag inside it, keeping attribute case and accepting
      `<![CDATA[…]]>`. The archimedes library renders a `Math` value into one.
  entry #Menu   `<menu>`: a toolbar's commands, over `<li>` elements; whitespace only between them
  entry #Meta   `<meta>`: document metadata; a void tag with a preset for every standard `name`
    detail
        Takes `name`, `content`, `charset` and `http-equiv` (as `httpEquiv`). Each member
        presets `name`, so `Meta.Viewport(content = t"width=device-width")` is
        `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width">`, and the parser reads
        `<meta name="viewport">` back as `Meta.Viewport`. Unlike `#Area`, `#Input`,
        `#Link` and `#Track`, this object does not fix its `Topic`, `Transport` and
        `Form` type members.
    entry .ApplicationName   `<meta name="application-name">`
    entry .Author   `<meta name="author">`
    entry .Description   `<meta name="description">`
    entry .Generator   `<meta name="generator">`
    entry .Keywords   `<meta name="keywords">`
    entry .Referrer   `<meta name="referrer">`
    entry .Viewport   `<meta name="viewport">`
    entry .ThemeColor   `<meta name="theme-color">`
    entry .ColorScheme   `<meta name="color-scheme">`
  entry #Meter   `<meter>`: a scalar measurement in a known range, over phrasing content
      Takes `value`, `min`, `max`, `low`, `high` and `optimum`, each a `Decimal`.
  entry #Nav   `<nav>`: navigation links, over flow content
  entry #Noscript   `<noscript>`: fallback for when scripting is off, over `<link>`, `<style>` and `<meta>`
      The vocabulary gives it the `<head>` form; the specification's flow-content form in a
      `<body>` is not typed.
  entry #Object   `<object>`: an external resource; transparent, with `data`, `type`, `name`, `form`, `width` and `height`
      A boundary tag for formatting elements.
  entry #Ol   `<ol>`: an ordered list, over `<li>` elements; whitespace only between them
      Takes `reversed`, `start` and `type`.
  entry #Optgroup   `<optgroup>`: a group of `<option>`s with a `<legend>`; autoclosing, whitespace between
      Takes `disabled` and `label`.
  entry #Option   `<option>`: a choice in a `<select>` or `<datalist>`, over text; autoclosing
      Takes `disabled`, `label`, `selected` and `value`. Shadows `scala.Option` within
      `import htmlDoms.whatwg.*`, which is one more reason to prefer `Optional`.
  entry #Output   `<output>`: the result of a calculation, over phrasing content; `for` names its inputs
  entry #P   `<p>`: a paragraph, over phrasing content; autoclosing
      Autoclosing is what lets `<p>one<p>two` parse as two paragraphs; `P(Div(…))` does not
      compile, since a `<div>` is not phrasing content.
  entry #Picture   `<picture>`: an image with alternative `<source>`s, over `<source>` and `<img>`; whitespace between
  entry #Pre   `<pre>`: preformatted text, over phrasing content
      Whitespace inside it is preserved by the serializer where elsewhere the indented
      formatting may reflow it.
  entry #Progress   `<progress>`: task completion, over phrasing content; `value` and `max`
  entry #Q   `<q>`: an inline quotation, over phrasing content; `cite` gives its source
  entry #Rp   `<rp>`: fallback parentheses for ruby annotations, over text; autoclosing
  entry #Rt   `<rt>`: a ruby annotation, over phrasing content; autoclosing
  entry #Ruby   `<ruby>`: ruby-annotated text, over phrasing content with `<rt>` and `<rp>`
  entry #S   `<s>`: text no longer accurate, over phrasing content
  entry #Samp   `<samp>`: sample output, over phrasing content
  entry #Script   `<script>`: a script, over raw text
      `Html.Mode.Raw`: the content is read to the first `</script>` without decoding
      entities or recognising tags, and written as it is. Takes `src`, `type`, `async`,
      `defer`, `nomodule`, `crossorigin`, `integrity`, `referrerpolicy`, `blocking` and
      `fetchpriority`.
  entry #Search   `<search>`: a search form or control group, over flow content
  entry #Section   `<section>`: a generic section, over flow content
  entry #Select   `<select>`: a drop-down control, over `<option>`, `<optgroup>`, `<hr>`, `<button>` and `<noscript>`
      Whitespace only between children. Takes `name`, `multiple`, `size`, `disabled`,
      `required`, `form` and `autocomplete`.
  entry #Selectedcontent   `<selectedcontent>`: the mirrored content of a customisable `<select>`'s chosen option; void
  entry #Slot   `<slot>`: a shadow-tree insertion point; transparent, with `name`
  entry #Small   `<small>`: side comments and fine print, over phrasing content
  entry #Source   `<source>`: a media or image source; void
      Takes `src`, `type`, `srcset`, `sizes`, `media`, `width` and `height`.
  entry #Span   `<span>`: a generic phrasing container
  entry #Strong   `<strong>`: strong importance, over phrasing content
  entry #Style   `<style>`: embedded CSS, over raw text
      `Html.Mode.Raw`: `Style(".a { color: red }")` writes its text as it is, with no entity
      encoding, and the parser decodes none. Takes `media` and `blocking`.
  entry #Sub   `<sub>`: a subscript, over phrasing content
  entry #Summary   `<summary>`: a `<details>`' caption, over phrasing content or one heading
  entry #Sup   `<sup>`: a superscript, over phrasing content
  entry #Svg   `<svg>`: SVG content, foreign to HTML
      A foreign tag: its children are `Html.Element.foreign` nodes, built by the parser for
      any tag inside it with attribute case kept and `<![CDATA[…]]>` accepted. Takes
      `width` and `height`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"""<svg><circle r="1"/></svg>""".read[Html of Flow]
      // Svg(Html.Element.foreign("circle", Map(t"r" -> t"1")))
      ```
  entry #Table   `<table>`: a table, over `<caption>`, `<colgroup>`, `<thead>`, `<tbody>` and `<tfoot>`
      Whitespace only between children, and a boundary for formatting elements. A `<tr>`
      given directly to a `Table(…)` does not compile; the parser, by contrast, infers the
      `<tbody>` (`#infer`), so `<table><tr>…` reads as `Table(Tbody(Tr(…)))`.
  entry #Tbody   `<tbody>`: a table's body rows, over `<tr>`; autoclosing, insertable, whitespace between
  entry #Td   `<td>`: a data cell, over flow content; autoclosing, and a boundary
      Takes `colspan`, `rowspan` and `headers`.
  entry #Template   `<template>`: an inert fragment, over flow and metadata content and every in-situ child
      Since its contents are never rendered in place, it admits what is otherwise valid only
      inside a list, table, row, ruby or `<select>`: `<li>`, `<tr>`, `<td>`, `<option>`,
      `<source>`, and so on. Serialized as `<template></template>` when empty; parsed up to
      a required `</template>`. Takes `shadowrootmode` and its `shadowroot…` siblings.
  entry #Textarea   `<textarea>`: a multi-line text control, over text
      `Html.Mode.Rcdata`: entities in its content are decoded but no tags recognised, so
      `<textarea><div>x</div></textarea>` reads as the text `<div>x</div>`. Takes `name`,
      `rows`, `cols`, `wrap`, `placeholder`, `maxlength`, `minlength`, `required`,
      `readonly`, `disabled`, `dirname`, `form` and `autocomplete`.
  entry #Tfoot   `<tfoot>`: a table's footer rows, over `<tr>`; autoclosing, whitespace between
  entry #Th   `<th>`: a header cell, over flow content; autoclosing, and a boundary
      Takes `colspan`, `rowspan`, `headers`, `scope` and `abbr`.
  entry #Thead   `<thead>`: a table's header rows, over `<tr>`; autoclosing, whitespace between
  entry #Time   `<time>`: a date or time, over phrasing content; `datetime` gives the machine form
  entry #Title   `<title>`: the document's title, over text; in `<head>`
      `Html.Mode.Rcdata`: `<title>a &amp; <b></title>` reads as the text `a & <b>`.
  entry #Tr   `<tr>`: a table row, over `<td>` and `<th>`; autoclosing, insertable, whitespace between
  entry #Track   `<track>`: a timed text track for `<audio>` or `<video>`; a void tag with a preset for every `kind`
    detail
        A `Tag.Void` whose `Topic` is `"track"`, taking `src`, `srclang`, `label`,
        `default` and `kind`. Each member presets `kind`.
    entry .Captions   `<track kind="captions">`
    entry .Chapters   `<track kind="chapters">`
    entry .Descriptions   `<track kind="descriptions">`
    entry .Metadata   `<track kind="metadata">`
    entry .Subtitles   `<track kind="subtitles">`
    entry .Topic   `"track"`
    entry .Transport   `""`: no children
    entry .Form   `Whatwg`
  entry #U   `<u>`: unarticulated annotation, over phrasing content
  entry #Ul   `<ul>`: an unordered list, over `<li>` elements; whitespace only between them
  entry #Var   `<var>`: a variable, over phrasing content
  entry #Video   `<video>`: a video; transparent, plus `<source>` and `<track>` children
      Takes `src`, `poster`, `crossorigin`, `preload`, `autoplay`, `playsinline`, `loop`,
      `muted`, `controls`, `width` and `height`.
  entry #Wbr   `<wbr>`: a line-break opportunity; void

entry .Html4Transitional   the HTML 4.01 Transitional dialect: its tags as a `Dom`, and its attributes as givens
  detail
      The second description of HTML honeycomb ships, for documents which must be HTML 4.01
      Transitional: legacy content, email templates, and markup which uses the
      presentational elements and attributes the Living Standard dropped. Like `Whatwg`, it
      is an **object** holding the attribute givens, and a **class** which is the `Dom`: the
      tag vocabulary (`Font`, `Center`, `Applet`, `Strike`, `Isindex`, … as well as the
      common tags), the HTML 4 content categories (`#Inline`, `#Block`, `#Flow`), the
      `HTML PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.01 Transitional//EN"` doctype, and the HTML 4
      character references.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.html4Transitional
      import htmlDoms.html4Transitional.*
      import attributives.textAttributive

      Html(Head(Title("Legacy")), Body(bgcolor = t"#ffffff")(Center(Font(color = t"red")("Hi"))))
      ```

      Its attribute value markers are the `Whatwg` ones (`Textual`, `Url`, `Presence`,
      `Color`, …), re-exported as aliases, so the same `Attributive` givens apply: a
      `Boolean` for `checked`, an `Int` for `colspan`, a `Text` for anything with
      `attributives.textAttributive`. The dialect's own markers (`Align`, `Charset`,
      `Clear`, `Frame`, `Rules`, `Scope`, `Size`, `Valign`, `Valuetype`, `Wrap`) are
      aliases of `Whatwg.Textual`. The intrinsic event attributes (`onclick`, `onload`, …)
      are typed `of Script`, an alias of `Whatwg.Scripting`, so a handler must be a typed
      scripting value rather than text.

      An attribute of this dialect is an `Attribute in Html4Transitional`, distinct from
      the WHATWG one, so a tag of one vocabulary never takes the other's attributes. The
      parser recognises the HTML 4 doctype, and `Html.Provenance(htmlDoms.html4Transitional)`
      is the metadata of a document loaded under it. Tags are `Tag.Container of "font" over
      Inline in Html4Transitional` and the like.
  refer honeycomb.Whatwg
  refer honeycomb.Dom
  refer honeycomb.Tag
  entry .Attribute   `honeycomb.Attribute in Html4Transitional`: an attribute of this dialect
  entry .attribute   makes the given for an attribute valid on named elements of this dialect
      As `Whatwg.attribute`, but typed `in Html4Transitional`:
      `attribute["bgcolor", "body" | "table", Color]()`.
  entry .globalAttribute   makes the given for an attribute valid on every element of this dialect
      As `Whatwg.globalAttribute`, but typed `in Html4Transitional`.
  entry .Color   `Whatwg.Color`: a colour value, as `bgcolor`, `text`, `link` and `color` take
  entry .Coords   `Whatwg.Coords`
  entry .Css   `Whatwg.Css`
  entry .CssClassList   `Whatwg.CssClassList`
  entry .Datetime   `Whatwg.Datetime`
  entry .Dir   `Whatwg.Dir`
  entry .Enctype   `Whatwg.Enctype`
  entry .HttpEquiv   `Whatwg.HttpEquiv`
  entry .Id   `Whatwg.Id`
  entry .Integral   `Whatwg.Integral`
  entry .Language   `Whatwg.Language`
  entry .MediaQueryList   `Whatwg.MediaQueryList`
  entry .Method   `Whatwg.Method`
  entry .MimeList   `Whatwg.MimeList`
  entry .Mime   `Whatwg.Mime`
  entry .Name   `Whatwg.Name`
  entry .OlType   `Whatwg.OlType`
  entry .Presence   `Whatwg.Presence`: a boolean attribute, present or absent
  entry .PositiveInt   `Whatwg.PositiveInt`
  entry .Regex   `Whatwg.Regex`
  entry .Shape   `Whatwg.Shape`
  entry .Target   `Whatwg.Target`
  entry .Textual   `Whatwg.Textual`: any text
  entry .Tokens   `Whatwg.Tokens`
  entry .Url   `Whatwg.Url`
  entry .Align   the value marker of `align`: `left`, `center`, `right` or `justify`; any text is accepted
  entry .Charset   the value marker of `charset` and `accept-charset`: a character encoding name; any text is accepted
  entry .Clear   the value marker of a `<br>`'s `clear`: `left`, `right`, `all` or `none`; any text is accepted
  entry .Frame   the value marker of a `<table>`'s `frame`: which sides of the border to draw; any text is accepted
  entry .Rules   the value marker of a `<table>`'s `rules`: which inner borders to draw; any text is accepted
  entry .Scope   the value marker of a cell's `scope`: `row`, `col`, `rowgroup` or `colgroup`; any text is accepted
  entry .Script   the value marker of an intrinsic event handler: `Whatwg.Scripting`, which plain text does not satisfy
      `onclick = t"…"` does not compile, deliberately: a handler is a typed scripting value
      for which a scripting library supplies the `Attributive to Whatwg.Scripting`.
  entry .Size   the value marker of a `<font>`, `<basefont>` or `<hr>`'s `size`; any text is accepted
  entry .Valign   the value marker of `valign`: `top`, `middle`, `bottom` or `baseline`; any text is accepted
  entry .Valuetype   the value marker of a `<param>`'s `valuetype`: `data`, `ref` or `object`; any text is accepted
  entry .Wrap   the value marker of a `<textarea>`'s `wrap`; any text is accepted
  entry .id   `id` on every element, as `Id`
  entry .class   `class` on every element, as `CssClassList`
  entry .style   `style` on every element, as `Css`
  entry .title   `title` on every element, as `Textual`
  entry .lang   `lang` on every element, as `Language`
  entry .dir   `dir` on every element, as `Dir`
  entry .onclick   `onclick` on every element, as `Script`
  entry .ondblclick   `ondblclick` on every element, as `Script`
  entry .onmousedown   `onmousedown` on every element, as `Script`
  entry .onmouseup   `onmouseup` on every element, as `Script`
  entry .onmouseover   `onmouseover` on every element, as `Script`
  entry .onmousemove   `onmousemove` on every element, as `Script`
  entry .onmouseout   `onmouseout` on every element, as `Script`
  entry .onkeypress   `onkeypress` on every element, as `Script`
  entry .onkeydown   `onkeydown` on every element, as `Script`
  entry .onkeyup   `onkeyup` on every element, as `Script`
  entry .onload   `onload` on `<body>` and `<frameset>`, as `Script`
  entry .onunload   `onunload` on `<body>` and `<frameset>`, as `Script`
  entry .onfocus   `onfocus` on `<a>`, `<area>`, `<label>`, `<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>` and `<button>`, as `Script`
  entry .onblur   `onblur` on `<a>`, `<area>`, `<label>`, `<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>` and `<button>`, as `Script`
  entry .onsubmit   `onsubmit` on `<form>`, as `Script`
  entry .onreset   `onreset` on `<form>`, as `Script`
  entry .onchange   `onchange` on `<input>`, `<select>` and `<textarea>`, as `Script`
  entry .onselect   `onselect` on `<input>` and `<textarea>`, as `Script`
  entry .href   `href` on `<a>`, `<area>`, `<base>` and `<link>`, as `Url`
  entry .src   `src` on `<img>`, `<input>`, `<script>` and `<iframe>`, as `Url`
  entry .alt   `alt` on `<applet>`, `<area>`, `<img>` and `<input>`, as `Textual`
  entry .type   `type` on `<a>`, `<link>`, `<object>`, `<param>`, `<script>`, `<style>`, `<input>` and `<button>`, as `Mime`
      Written as `` `type` = … `` since `type` is a keyword; an `<input>`'s type is usually
      chosen through `#Input`'s presets instead.
  entry .typeOl   `type` on `<ol>`, `<ul>` and `<li>`: the list marker style, as `OlType`
  entry .name   `name` on `<a>`, `<applet>`, `<button>`, `<form>`, `<iframe>`, `<img>`, `<input>`, `<map>`, `<meta>`, `<object>`, `<param>`, `<select>` and `<textarea>`
      Of `Name`. An `<a name=…>` is the HTML 4 anchor, which the Living Standard replaced by
      `id`.
  entry .value   `value` on `<input>`, `<option>`, `<param>` and `<button>`, as `Textual`
  entry .valueLi   `value` on `<li>`: the item's number, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .rel   `rel` on `<a>` and `<link>`, as `Tokens`
  entry .rev   `rev` on `<a>` and `<link>`: the reverse link type, as `Tokens`
      The `honeycomb.Rev` enum has the HTML 4 link types, passed as `.show`.
  entry .target   `target` on `<a>`, `<area>`, `<base>`, `<form>` and `<link>`, as `Target`
  entry .action   `action` on `<form>`, as `Url`
  entry .method   `method` on `<form>`, as `Method`
  entry .enctype   `enctype` on `<form>`, as `Enctype`
  entry .accept   `accept` on `<form>` and `<input>`, as `MimeList`
  entry .acceptCharset   `accept-charset` on `<form>`, as `Charset`
      Written `acceptCharset = …`; the attribute serializes as `accept-charset`.
  entry .checked   `checked` on `<input>`: a `Presence`
  entry .disabled   `disabled` on `<button>`, `<input>`, `<optgroup>`, `<option>`, `<select>` and `<textarea>`: a `Presence`
  entry .readonly   `readonly` on `<input>` and `<textarea>`: a `Presence`
  entry .multiple   `multiple` on `<select>`: a `Presence`
  entry .selected   `selected` on `<option>`: a `Presence`
  entry .maxlength   `maxlength` on `<input>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .size   `size` on `<input>` and `<select>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .sizeFont   `size` on `<basefont>`, `<font>` and `<hr>`: a font size (`1` to `7`, or `+1`) or a rule height, as `Size`
  entry .rows   `rows` on `<textarea>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .cols   `cols` on `<textarea>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .wrap   `wrap` on `<textarea>`, as `Wrap`
  entry .for   `for` on `<label>`: the id of the labelled control, as `Id`
      Written as `` `for` = … `` since `for` is a keyword.
  entry .usemap   `usemap` on `<img>`, `<input>` and `<object>`: the image map, as `Url`
  entry .ismap   `ismap` on `<img>` and `<input>`: a `Presence`
  entry .longdesc   `longdesc` on `<img>` and `<iframe>`: a long description's URL, as `Url`
  entry .shape   `shape` on `<a>` and `<area>`, as `Shape`
  entry .coords   `coords` on `<a>` and `<area>`, as `Coords`
  entry .nohref   `nohref` on `<area>`: a `Presence`
  entry .hreflang   `hreflang` on `<a>` and `<link>`, as `Language`
  entry .charset   `charset` on `<a>`, `<link>`, `<script>` and `<meta>`, as `Charset`
  entry .media   `media` on `<link>` and `<style>`, as `MediaQueryList`
  entry .defer   `defer` on `<script>`: a `Presence`
  entry .language   `language` on `<script>`: the scripting language's name, as `Textual`
  entry .httpEquiv   `http-equiv` on `<meta>`, as `HttpEquiv`
      Written `httpEquiv = …`; the attribute serializes as `http-equiv`.
  entry .content   `content` on `<meta>`, as `Textual`
  entry .scheme   `scheme` on `<meta>`: the scheme interpreting `content`, as `Textual`
  entry .cite   `cite` on `<blockquote>`, `<del>`, `<ins>` and `<q>`, as `Url`
  entry .datetime   `datetime` on `<del>` and `<ins>`, as `Datetime`
  entry .accesskey   `accesskey` on `<a>`, `<area>`, `<button>`, `<input>`, `<label>`, `<legend>` and `<textarea>`, as `Textual`
  entry .tabindex   `tabindex` on `<a>`, `<area>`, `<button>`, `<input>`, `<object>`, `<select>` and `<textarea>`, as `Integral`
  entry .profile   `profile` on `<head>`: a metadata profile URL, as `Url`
  entry .version   `version` on `<html>`: the DTD version, as `Textual`
  entry .prompt   `prompt` on `<isindex>`: the prompt text, as `Textual`
  entry .xmlns   `xmlns` on `<html>`: the XHTML namespace, as `Url`
  entry .label   `label` on `<optgroup>` and `<option>`, as `Textual`
  entry .standby   `standby` on `<object>`: text shown while loading, as `Textual`
  entry .classid   `classid` on `<object>`: the implementation's URL, as `Url`
