  entry .codetype   `codetype` on `<object>`: the implementation's MIME type, as `Mime`
  entry .archive   `archive` on `<applet>` and `<object>`: archive URLs, as `Url`
  entry .declare   `declare` on `<object>`: a `Presence`
  entry .data   `data` on `<object>`: the resource URL, as `Url`
  entry .code   `code` on `<applet>`: the applet class file, as `Textual`
  entry .mayscript   `mayscript` on `<applet>`: a `Presence`
  entry .object   `object` on `<applet>`: a serialized applet, as `Textual`
      Written as `` `object` = … `` since `object` is a keyword.
  entry .valuetype   `valuetype` on `<param>`, as `Valuetype`
  entry .frameborder   `frameborder` on `<iframe>`: `0` or `1`, as `Integral`
  entry .scrolling   `scrolling` on `<iframe>`: `yes`, `no` or `auto`, as `Textual`
  entry .marginheight   `marginheight` on `<iframe>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .marginwidth   `marginwidth` on `<iframe>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .summary   `summary` on `<table>`: a description for non-visual rendering, as `Textual`
  entry .border   `border` on `<img>`, `<object>` and `<table>`: the border width, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .cellpadding   `cellpadding` on `<table>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .cellspacing   `cellspacing` on `<table>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .frameTable   `frame` on `<table>`: which outer borders to draw, as `Frame`
  entry .rules   `rules` on `<table>`: which inner borders to draw, as `Rules`
  entry .colspan   `colspan` on `<td>` and `<th>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .rowspan   `rowspan` on `<td>` and `<th>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .headers   `headers` on `<td>` and `<th>`: the ids of header cells, as `Tokens`
  entry .abbr   `abbr` on `<td>` and `<th>`, as `Textual`
  entry .axis   `axis` on `<td>` and `<th>`: the cell's categories, as `Textual`
  entry .scope   `scope` on `<td>` and `<th>`, as `Scope`
  entry .valign   `valign` on `<col>`, `<colgroup>`, `<tbody>`, `<td>`, `<tfoot>`, `<th>`, `<thead>` and `<tr>`, as `Valign`
  entry .char   `char` on `<col>`, `<colgroup>`, `<tbody>`, `<td>`, `<tfoot>`, `<th>`, `<thead>` and `<tr>`: the alignment character, as `Textual`
  entry .charoff   `charoff` on the same elements as `char`: the offset to the alignment character, as `Textual`
  entry .span   `span` on `<col>` and `<colgroup>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .start   `start` on `<ol>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .compact   `compact` on `<dir>`, `<dl>`, `<menu>`, `<ol>` and `<ul>`: a `Presence`
  entry .align   `align` on captions, columns, headings, `<div>`, `<p>`, `<hr>`, `<img>`, `<table>` and its parts, and more, as `Align`
      On `<applet>`, `<caption>`, `<col>`, `<colgroup>`, `<div>`, `<h1>` to `<h6>`, `<hr>`,
      `<iframe>`, `<img>`, `<input>`, `<legend>`, `<object>`, `<p>`, `<table>`, `<tbody>`,
      `<td>`, `<tfoot>`, `<th>`, `<thead>` and `<tr>`.
  entry .bgcolor   `bgcolor` on `<body>`, `<table>`, `<td>`, `<th>` and `<tr>`, as `Color`
  entry .background   `background` on `<body>`: a background image, as `Url`
  entry .text   `text` on `<body>`: the text colour, as `Color`
  entry .link   `link` on `<body>`: the unvisited link colour, as `Color`
  entry .vlink   `vlink` on `<body>`: the visited link colour, as `Color`
  entry .alink   `alink` on `<body>`: the active link colour, as `Color`
  entry .color   `color` on `<basefont>` and `<font>`, as `Color`
  entry .face   `face` on `<basefont>` and `<font>`: a font family list, as `Textual`
  entry .noshade   `noshade` on `<hr>`: a `Presence`
  entry .clear   `clear` on `<br>`: which floats to move past, as `Clear`
  entry .width   `width` on `<applet>`, `<col>`, `<colgroup>`, `<hr>`, `<iframe>`, `<img>`, `<object>`, `<pre>`, `<table>`, `<td>` and `<th>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .height   `height` on `<applet>`, `<iframe>`, `<img>`, `<object>`, `<td>` and `<th>`, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .hspace   `hspace` on `<applet>`, `<img>` and `<object>`: horizontal margin, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .vspace   `vspace` on `<applet>`, `<img>` and `<object>`: vertical margin, as `PositiveInt`
  entry .nowrap   `nowrap` on `<td>` and `<th>`: a `Presence`
  entry #doctype   the HTML 4.01 Transitional doctype, with its public identifier and the loose DTD's URL
      `<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.01 Transitional//EN"
      "http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/loose.dtd">`, which a `Document[Html]` loaded or built
      under this dialect carries.
  entry #Heading   `<h1>` to `<h6>`
  entry #Phrase   the HTML 4 phrase elements: `<em>`, `<strong>`, `<dfn>`, `<code>`, `<samp>`, `<kbd>`, `<var>`, `<cite>`, `<abbr>` and `<acronym>`
  entry #FontStyle   the HTML 4 font-style elements: `<tt>`, `<i>`, `<b>`, `<u>`, `<s>`, `<strike>`, `<big>` and `<small>`
  entry #Special   the HTML 4 special inline elements: links, images, objects, fonts, breaks, scripts, maps, quotes and the rest
      `<a>`, `<img>`, `<applet>`, `<object>`, `<font>`, `<basefont>`, `<br>`, `<script>`, `<map>`,
      `<q>`, `<sub>`, `<sup>`, `<span>`, `<bdo>` and `<iframe>`.
  entry #FormCtrl   the HTML 4 form controls: `<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>`, `<label>` and `<button>`
  entry #Inline   the inline content category: phrase, font-style, special and form-control elements, and text
      The HTML 4 counterpart of WHATWG's `Phrasing`; includes `"#text"`.
  entry #Lists   the list elements: `<ul>`, `<ol>`, `<dir>` and `<menu>`
  entry #Block   the block-level content category
      Headings, lists, `<p>`, `<pre>`, `<dl>`, `<div>`, `<noscript>`, `<blockquote>`,
      `<form>`, `<hr>`, `<table>`, `<fieldset>`, `<address>`, `<center>`, `<isindex>` and
      `<noframes>`.
  entry #Flow   the flow content category: `#Block` or `#Inline`
      The type for a piece of body content: `Html of Flow`.
  entry #Metadata   what a `<head>` may contain: `<title>`, `<base>`, `<script>`, `<style>`, `<meta>`, `<link>` and `<object>`
  entry #insertable   the tags which the parser may insert unwritten between a tag and a child
      As `Whatwg#insertable`: `<head>`, `<body>`, `<tbody>`, `<tr>` and `<colgroup>`.
  entry #infer   the tag to insert between a parent and a child the parent cannot hold directly
      As `Whatwg#infer`; the `Dom` contract the parser calls.
  entry #elements   every tag of the dialect, by its name
  entry #entities   the HTML 4 named character references, by name
      The 252 entities of the HTML 4 DTDs; the Living Standard's additions are not included.
  entry #attributes   every attribute of the dialect, by its name, the several givens for one name merged
  entry #Html   the `<html>` root element, as `honeycomb.Html`
      A lazy alias so that `import htmlDoms.html4Transitional.*` brings `Html` into scope as
      a tag: `Html(Head(Title("x")), Body(P("y")))`.
  entry #A   `<a>`: a hyperlink or, with `name`, an anchor; transparent
  entry #Abbr   `<abbr>`: an abbreviation, over inline content
  entry #Acronym   `<acronym>`: an acronym, over inline content; not in the Living Standard
  entry #Address   `<address>`: contact information, over inline content and `<p>`
  entry #Applet   `<applet>`: a Java applet, over `<param>` and flow content; deprecated in HTML 4
      Takes `code`, `codebase`, `archive`, `object`, `alt`, `name`, `width`, `height`,
      `align`, `hspace`, `vspace` and `mayscript`.
  entry #Area   `<area>`: a region of an image map; void
  entry #B   `<b>`: bold text, over inline content
  entry #Base   `<base>`: the document base URL; void
  entry #Basefont   `<basefont>`: the base font size, colour and face; void, deprecated in HTML 4
  entry #Bdo   `<bdo>`: text with an explicit direction, over inline content
  entry #Big   `<big>`: larger text, over inline content; not in the Living Standard
  entry #Blockquote   `<blockquote>`: a long quotation, over flow content
  entry #Body   `<body>`: the document's content, over flow content; autoclosing and insertable
      Takes the presentational `bgcolor`, `background`, `text`, `link`, `vlink` and `alink`,
      and `onload` and `onunload`.
  entry #Br   `<br>`: a line break; void; `clear` moves past floats
  entry #Button   `<button>`: a button, over flow content; a boundary for formatting elements
  entry #Caption   `<caption>`: a table's caption, over inline content; a boundary
  entry #Center   `<center>`: centred flow content; deprecated in HTML 4, absent from the Living Standard
  entry #Cite   `<cite>`: a citation, over inline content
  entry #Code   `<code>`: computer code, over inline content
  entry #Col   `<col>`: a table column; void
  entry #Colgroup   `<colgroup>`: a group of columns, over `<col>`; whitespace between, insertable
  entry #Dd   `<dd>`: a definition in a `<dl>`, over flow content; autoclosing
  entry #Del   `<del>`: deleted text; transparent
  entry #Dfn   `<dfn>`: a defining instance, over inline content
  entry #Dir   `<dir>`: a directory list, over `<li>`; deprecated in HTML 4
      Whitespace only between items. Not to be confused with the `dir` attribute or the
      `HDir` enum.
  entry #Div   `<div>`: a generic block container, over flow content; `align` is permitted
  entry #Dl   `<dl>`: a definition list, over `<dt>` and `<dd>`; autoclosing, whitespace between
  entry #Dt   `<dt>`: a term in a `<dl>`, over inline content; autoclosing
  entry #Em   `<em>`: emphasis, over inline content
  entry #Fieldset   `<fieldset>`: a group of controls: an optional `<legend>` then flow content
  entry #Font   `<font>`: text with a `color`, `face` or `size`, over inline content; deprecated in HTML 4
  entry #Form   `<form>`: a form, over flow content
      Takes `action`, `method`, `enctype`, `accept`, `accept-charset` (as `acceptCharset`),
      `name`, `target`, `onsubmit` and `onreset`.
  entry #H1   `<h1>`: a heading of the first rank, over inline content
  entry #H2   `<h2>`: a heading of the second rank, over inline content
  entry #H3   `<h3>`: a heading of the third rank, over inline content
  entry #H4   `<h4>`: a heading of the fourth rank, over inline content
  entry #H5   `<h5>`: a heading of the fifth rank, over inline content
  entry #H6   `<h6>`: a heading of the sixth rank, over inline content
  entry #Head   `<head>`: the document's metadata, over `#Metadata`; autoclosing, insertable, whitespace between
  entry #Hr   `<hr>`: a horizontal rule; void; takes `align`, `noshade`, `size` and `width`
  entry #I   `<i>`: italic text, over inline content
  entry #Iframe   `<iframe>`: an inline frame, over flow content as fallback
      A container in this dialect, unlike `Whatwg#Iframe`. Takes `src`, `name`, `longdesc`,
      `frameborder`, `scrolling`, `marginheight`, `marginwidth`, `width`, `height` and
      `align`.
  entry #Img   `<img>`: an image; void
      Takes `src`, `alt`, `longdesc`, `name`, `usemap`, `ismap`, `width`, `height`,
      `border`, `align`, `hspace` and `vspace`.
  entry #Input   `<input>`: a form control; a void tag with a preset for every HTML 4 `type`
    detail
        A `Tag.Void` whose `Topic` is `"input"`. Each member presets `type`;
        `Input.Checkbox(name = t"terms", checked = true)` is
        `<input type="checkbox" name="terms" checked>`.
    entry .Hidden   `<input type="hidden">`
    entry .Text   `<input type="text">`
    entry .Password   `<input type="password">`
    entry .Checkbox   `<input type="checkbox">`
    entry .Radio   `<input type="radio">`
    entry .Submit   `<input type="submit">`
    entry .Image   `<input type="image">`
    entry .Reset   `<input type="reset">`
    entry .Button   `<input type="button">`
    entry .File   `<input type="file">`
    entry .Topic   `"input"`
    entry .Transport   `""`: no children
    entry .Form   `Html4Transitional`
  entry #Ins   `<ins>`: inserted text; transparent
  entry #Isindex   `<isindex>`: a single-line search prompt; void, deprecated in HTML 4
  entry #Kbd   `<kbd>`: keyboard input, over inline content
  entry #Label   `<label>`: a control's caption, over inline content
  entry #Legend   `<legend>`: a `<fieldset>`'s caption, over inline content
  entry #Li   `<li>`: a list item, over flow content; autoclosing
  entry #Link   `<link>`: a link to an external resource; void
  entry #Map   `<map>`: an image map, over block content and `<area>`s
  entry #Menu   `<menu>`: a menu list, over `<li>`; deprecated in HTML 4; whitespace between
  entry #Meta   `<meta>`: document metadata; void
  entry #Noframes   `<noframes>`: content for agents without frames, over flow content
  entry #Noscript   `<noscript>`: content for agents without scripting, over flow content
  entry #Object   `<object>`: an embedded object, over `<param>` and flow content; a boundary
  entry #Ol   `<ol>`: an ordered list, over `<li>`; whitespace between; takes `start`, `type` and `compact`
  entry #Optgroup   `<optgroup>`: a group of options, over `<option>`; autoclosing, whitespace between
  entry #Option   `<option>`: a choice in a `<select>`, over text; autoclosing
      Shadows `scala.Option` within `import htmlDoms.html4Transitional.*`.
  entry #P   `<p>`: a paragraph, over inline content; autoclosing
  entry #Param   `<param>`: a parameter for an `<object>` or `<applet>`; void
  entry #Pre   `<pre>`: preformatted text, over inline content; `width` is permitted
  entry #Q   `<q>`: a short quotation, over inline content
  entry #S   `<s>`: struck-through text, over inline content; deprecated in HTML 4
  entry #Samp   `<samp>`: sample output, over inline content
  entry #Script   `<script>`: a script, over raw text
      `Html.Mode.Raw`: read to `</script>` with no entity decoding. Takes `src`, `type`,
      `language`, `charset` and `defer`.
  entry #Select   `<select>`: a menu control, over `<option>` and `<optgroup>`; whitespace between
  entry #Small   `<small>`: smaller text, over inline content
  entry #Span   `<span>`: a generic inline container
  entry #Strike   `<strike>`: struck-through text, over inline content; deprecated in HTML 4
  entry #Strong   `<strong>`: strong emphasis, over inline content
  entry #Style   `<style>`: embedded CSS, over raw text; takes `type` and `media`
  entry #Sub   `<sub>`: a subscript, over inline content
  entry #Sup   `<sup>`: a superscript, over inline content
  entry #Table   `<table>`: a table, over `<caption>`, `<colgroup>`, `<thead>`, `<tbody>` and `<tfoot>`
      Whitespace only between children, and a boundary. Takes `summary`, `border`,
      `cellpadding`, `cellspacing`, `frame` (as `frameTable`), `rules`, `width`, `align` and
      `bgcolor`.
  entry #Tbody   `<tbody>`: body rows, over `<tr>`; autoclosing, insertable, whitespace between
  entry #Td   `<td>`: a data cell, over flow content; autoclosing, a boundary
      Takes `colspan`, `rowspan`, `headers`, `abbr`, `axis`, `scope`, `align`, `valign`,
      `char`, `charoff`, `bgcolor`, `width`, `height` and `nowrap`.
  entry #Textarea   `<textarea>`: a multi-line text control, over text (`Html.Mode.Rcdata`)
      Takes `name`, `rows`, `cols`, `wrap`, `readonly`, `disabled`, `accesskey`, `tabindex`
      and the `onfocus`, `onblur`, `onselect` and `onchange` handlers.
  entry #Tfoot   `<tfoot>`: footer rows, over `<tr>`; autoclosing, whitespace between
  entry #Th   `<th>`: a header cell, over flow content; autoclosing, a boundary; takes what `<td>` takes
  entry #Thead   `<thead>`: header rows, over `<tr>`; autoclosing, whitespace between
  entry #Title   `<title>`: the document's title, over text (`Html.Mode.Rcdata`)
  entry #Tr   `<tr>`: a table row, over `<td>` and `<th>`; autoclosing, insertable, whitespace between
  entry #Tt   `<tt>`: teletype text, over inline content; not in the Living Standard
  entry #U   `<u>`: underlined text, over inline content; deprecated in HTML 4
  entry #Ul   `<ul>`: an unordered list, over `<li>`; whitespace between; takes `type` and `compact`
  entry #Var   `<var>`: a variable, over inline content

entry .Tag   an HTML tag as a value: applied to attributes and children, it makes an element
  detail
      A `Tag` is the builder for one element name, and every `val` of a `Dom` such as
      `Whatwg` — `Div`, `P`, `Img`, `Table` — is one. It is itself an `Html.Element` (the
      empty element of its name, which is why `Br` alone is `<br>` and `Title` alone
      `<title></title>`), and applying it makes a new element: named arguments are
      attributes, positional arguments are children, and attributes come first.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
      import attributives.textAttributive

      Br                                           // <br>
      Img(src = t"image.jpg")                      // <img src="image.jpg">
      Div(Hr, P("Hello world"))                    // <div><hr><p>Hello world</p></div>
      Textarea(name = t"details", rows = 5)("Details go here")
      ```

      The checking is in the types. A tag's type is `Tag.Container of "ul" over "li" in
      Whatwg`, `Tag.Void of "br" in Whatwg` or `Tag.Transparent of "a" over "" in Whatwg`:
      its `Topic` is the element name, its `Transport` what it may contain, and its `Form` the
      dialect. A child must be an `Html of (? <: Transport)` — a `Tag`, an element, a `Text`
      or `String` (where text is admitted), an `Html.Fragment`, or any value with a
      `Renderable` into an admitted label — so `Ul("hello")` and `P(Li)` do not compile. An
      attribute is checked by name against the `Attribute` givens of the dialect's companion,
      and its value against the attribute's marker through `Attributive`, so `Br(Hr)`,
      `P(src = …)` and `Img(width = 1.5)` are all compile errors.

      A tag is `Dynamic` in two ways. `Tag(…)` and `Tag(attribute = …)` go through
      `#applyDynamicNamed`, where an `attributes` macro resolves each name. `Tag.name(…)`
      — `Div.panel(P("x"))` — goes through `Tag.Container#applyDynamic`, and makes an
      element whose `class` (or `id`) is `name`, provided an `Attribution of "panel"` is in
      scope: `stylesheets.uncheckedClasses` allows any name, and a cataclysm stylesheet
      allows only the names it declares. A preset class on the tag is kept and the new one
      appended.

      Besides the three subclasses, `Tag.root` makes the parser's synthetic root and
      `Tag.foreign` a tag for non-HTML content (`Svg`, `Math`). The flags `autoclose`,
      `mode`, `insertable`, `boundary`, `void` and `transparent` drive the parser and
      serializer, and `admissible` is the runtime form of `Transport`.
  refer honeycomb.Whatwg
  refer honeycomb.Attribute
  refer honeycomb.Attribution
  refer honeycomb.Dom
  entry .focusable   how a tag is named in a diagnostic: by its tag name
      A `Focusable` given for every tag's singleton type (the typeclass's `Self` is
      invariant, and each tag has a type of its own), used by a browser driver such as
      tarantula to locate elements by tag name (`driver \ Div`).
  entry .optical   an optic from an element to each of its children with this tag's name
      `Tag is Optical from html onto Html.Node`: `element.focus(Div)` (through panopticon)
      addresses every direct child whose label is the tag's, so a lens update maps over just
      those children and rebuilds the element around them. Other children are left as they
      are, and a non-element origin is returned unchanged.
  entry .root   a synthetic root tag admitting the given labels; for the parser
      `Tag.root(Set(t"html"))` is the invisible parent the parser reads into when the
      caller asks for `Html of "html"`; `read[Html of "ul" | "ol"]` uses one admitting both.
      Its `node` returns itself.
  entry .void   a void tag of a given label, with optional preset attributes
      `Tag.void["br", Whatwg]()` is a `Tag.Void of "br" in Whatwg`. `presets` are
      attributes every element made from the tag carries, which is how `Input.Email` is an
      `<input>` with `type="email"` fixed; `boundary` marks it a boundary for formatting
      elements.
  entry .foreign   a tag for non-HTML content, whose children are foreign elements
      Two forms. `Tag.foreign["svg", Whatwg]()` makes a `Container of "svg" over "#foreign"`,
      which `Svg` and `Math` are: inside it the parser builds `Html.Element.foreign` nodes
      for any tag, keeping attribute case and accepting CDATA. `Tag.foreign[dom](label,
      attributes)` makes a foreign tag of any label with attributes already set, typed
      `Tag of "#foreign" over "#foreign"`, as the parser does for `<circle>` inside `<svg>`.
  entry .container   a container tag of a given label over a union of child labels
      `Tag.container["ul", "li" | ScriptSupporting, Whatwg](mode = Html.Mode.Whitespace)`
      is a `Container of "ul" over "li" | "script" | "template" in Whatwg`. The children type
      is reified to the `admissible` set. `autoclose` lets the parser close the element when
      a sibling starts; `mode` is `Html.Mode.Normal`, `Whitespace` (only whitespace between
      children), `Raw` (`<script>`) or `Rcdata` (`<textarea>`, `<title>`); `insertable` lets
      the parser supply the element unwritten; `boundary` stops formatting elements
      crossing it; `presets` fixes attributes.
  entry .transparent   a transparent tag: holds what its parent may hold, plus named extras
      `Tag.transparent["a", "", Whatwg]()` is a `Transparent of "a" over "" in Whatwg`: when
      applied, its children are checked against the *enclosing* element's content model
      rather than its own, which is what makes `A` usable in both a `<p>` and a `<div>`. The
      children type names extras the tag itself admits besides what it inherits (`"source"
      | "track"` for `Video`). A second overload takes the label and admissible set as
      values.
  entry .Container   a tag which holds children
    detail
        The class of every ordinary tag: a `Container of label over children in dom`,
        whose `#Result` is an `Html.Element & Html.Vacuiscible` — an element to which
        children may still be supplied in a second argument list, `Div(id = t"x")(P("y"))`.
    entry #applyDynamic   `Tag.name(children*)`: an element with `name` as its class or id
        Dispatches `Div.panel(P("x"))` to make `<div class="panel"><p>x</p></div>`. The
        name is checked by the `Attribution of (? >: name)` in scope: `Attribution.empty`
        covers the plain `Div(…)` form (`apply`), `stylesheets.uncheckedClasses` permits any
        class name, and a stylesheet's own `Attribution` permits only its names, and makes
        an id of a name it declares as one. A preset `class` on the tag is kept, the new name
        appended after a space.
    entry #node   the element for a set of attributes, with no children yet
        The `Result` of applying attributes: an `Html.Element` of the tag's label, with its
        presets followed by the attributes given, and a `Vacuiscible#apply` through which
        children follow.
    entry #Result   `Html.Element & Html.Vacuiscible of Topic over Transport in Form`
  entry .Transparent   a tag which takes its content model from its parent
    detail
        The class of `A`, `Del`, `Ins`, `Canvas`, `Map`, `Object`, `Slot`, `Audio` and
        `Video`: its `#Result` is an `Html.Element & Html.Transparent`, whose `apply` takes
        children of any label union and records that union as the element's `Topic`, so the
        enclosing element then checks them as if they were its own.
    entry #applyDynamic   `Tag.name(children*)`: an element with `name` as its class or id
        As `Tag.Container#applyDynamic`, but the children are checked against the tag's own
        extras only; see `Tag.transparent`.
    entry #node   the element for a set of attributes, with no children yet
    entry #Result   `Html.Element & Html.Transparent of Topic over Transport in Form`
  entry .Void   a tag which takes no children
    detail
        The class of `Br`, `Img`, `Input`, `Link`, `Meta` and the rest. Its `#Result` is a
        plain `Html.Element of Topic in Form`, with no second argument list, so `Br(Hr)` does
        not compile. The objects `Whatwg.Input`, `Whatwg.Link`, `Whatwg.Meta`, `Whatwg.Area`
        and `Whatwg.Track` extend it directly in order to carry their preset variants.
    entry #node   the element for a set of attributes
    entry #Result   `Html.Element of Topic in Form`
  entry #autoclose   whether the parser may close this element when a sibling or parent closes
      `<li>`, `<p>`, `<dt>`, `<dd>`, `<option>`, `<tr>`, `<td>`, `<th>`, the table sections,
      `<head>` and `<body>`: the elements whose end tags HTML lets a document omit.
  entry #mode   how the element's content is read and written: `Html.Mode.Normal`, `Whitespace`, `Raw` or `Rcdata`
      `Raw` (`<script>`, `<style>`) reads to the end tag without decoding; `Rcdata`
      (`<textarea>`, `<title>`) decodes entities but no tags; `Whitespace` (lists, tables,
      `<select>`, `<head>`) allows only whitespace text between children, and the parser
      rejects other text with `Html.Issue.OnlyWhitespace`.
  entry #presets   the attributes every element made from this tag carries
      `type="email"` for `Input.Email`, `rel="stylesheet"` for `Link.Stylesheet`. Applying
      the tag adds its attributes after them, and the parser matches a tag's presets against
      a start tag's attributes to read `<input type="button">` back as `Input.Button`.
  entry #admissible   the labels this tag may contain, as a set of text
      The runtime form of `Transport`, used by the parser to reject a child
      (`Html.Issue.InadmissibleTag`) and by `Dom#infer` to find an element to insert. Empty
      for a void or purely transparent tag.
  entry #insertable   whether the parser may supply this element unwritten
      True for `<head>`, `<body>`, `<tbody>`, `<tr>` and `<colgroup>`.
  entry #void   whether the tag is void: no children and no end tag
  entry #transparent   whether the tag's content model is its parent's
  entry #boundary   whether formatting elements opened outside may not continue inside
      True for `<button>`, `<caption>`, `<object>`, `<table>`, `<td>`, `<th>` and
      `<template>`: the adoption-agency algorithm does not reconstruct an open `<b>` or
      `<i>` across one.
  entry #tableLike   whether this is `<table>`, `<tbody>`, `<thead>`, `<tfoot>` or `<tr>`
      Computed once when the tag is made, for the parser's foster-parenting checks.
  entry #isTable   whether this is `<table>`
  entry #Result   the type of element the tag makes: fixed by `Container`, `Transparent` and `Void`
  entry #applyDynamicNamed   `Tag(attribute = value, …)`: an element with those attributes
      The `Dynamic` entry point behind every application with named arguments. For the
      plain `apply` it builds the element from the pairs; for `Tag.name(attribute = value)`
      it also sets `name` as the class or id the `Attribution` in scope assigns. Each pair is
      handed to `#element`, whose macro resolves the attribute and converts the value.
  entry #element   the macro which checks each attribute and makes the element
      For every `(name, value)` pair, finds the `name is Attribute on (? >: Topic) in Form`
      given (or a global one), reads its `Topic` marker, and summons `value is Attributive
      to marker`, halting at the argument's position with _`name` has attribute type `marker`, but
      `value` cannot be attributed as a `marker` without a contextual instance of `value is
      Attributive to marker`_, or _the attribute `name` cannot be used on the element
      `<tag>`_ when no given admits the name on that element. A value whose `Attributive`
      yields `Unset` (a `false` `Boolean`, an empty class list) omits the attribute.
  entry #node   the element for a set of already-converted attributes; implemented by each subclass

entry .Attribute   an attribute of a dialect: its name, the elements it is valid on, and its value type
  detail
      `Attribute(label, elements, global)` is what every attribute given of `Whatwg` and
      `Html4Transitional` is an instance of. Its refinement does the typing: a given is a
      `"src" is Attribute on "img" | "video" of Url in Whatwg`, where `Self` is the name as a
      literal type, `Plane` the elements, `Topic` the value marker and `Form` the dialect.
      `Tag#element` resolves an attribute by summoning on `Self`, `Plane` and `Form`;
      `Html.Element#selectDynamic` does the same to read one back, through `Unattributive`.

      A dialect's `attributes` dictionary holds one `Attribute` per name, with the several
      givens for a name (`href` on `<a>`, on `<base>`, on `<link>`) merged by `#merge`.
      A program defines its own with `Whatwg.attribute()` or `Whatwg.globalAttribute()`.
  refer honeycomb.Whatwg
  refer honeycomb.Attributive
  entry #label   the attribute's name, as written in the markup
  entry #elements   the element names the attribute is valid on; empty for a global attribute
  entry #global   whether the attribute is valid on every element
  entry #targets   whether the attribute is valid on a named element
      `global || elements.has(tag)`; the parser's check for an unknown attribute.
  entry #merge   this attribute and another of the same name as one, valid on the union of their elements
  entry #Self   the attribute's name, as a string literal type
  entry #Topic   the marker of the values the attribute takes: `Whatwg.Url`, `Whatwg.Presence`, …
  entry #Plane   the element names the attribute is valid on, as a union of literal types

entry .Attribution   evidence that a name used as a method on a tag is a valid class or id
  detail
      `Div.panel(…)` is permitted only if an `Attribution of "panel"` (or of a union
      including it) is in scope; the attribution says which attribute the name populates,
      `class` or `id`. The library provides `.empty` for the plain `apply` form, and
      `stylesheets.uncheckedClasses` for any name; a cataclysm stylesheet brings its own,
      listing the classes and ids it declares, so that a name it does not know is a compile
      error. `.classes` makes one by hand for a known set of names.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      given (Attribution of "test" | "foo") = Attribution.classes()
      Table.test(Thead(Tr(Th("First")))).show   // <table class="test">…
      ```
  refer honeycomb.Tag
  refer honeycomb.ClassList
  entry .classes   an attribution of one or more names as classes
      `Attribution.classes[topic]()`, with `topic` a literal type or a union of them, as the
      `Topic`. The names are not checked against CSS identifier rules; `ClassList` is.
  entry .empty   the attribution of the plain `Tag(children)` call
      `Tag(…)` dispatches through `Dynamic` as `applyDynamic("apply")`, and this given, with
      an empty `attribute`, is what makes that form add nothing. In implicit scope.
  entry #attribute   the attribute the name populates: `class`, `id`, or empty for the plain call

entry .Attributive   how a value becomes an attribute's text, for an attribute's value marker
  detail
      `value is Attributive to marker` converts a value for an attribute whose `Topic` is
      `marker`: `Int is Attributive to Whatwg.PositiveInt` is why `Img(width = 50)`
      compiles and `Img(width = 1.5)` does not. `#attribute` takes the attribute's name and
      the value and returns the name with its text, the name alone (a bare attribute such as
      `disabled`), or `Unset`, which omits the attribute.

      The companion holds the givens for the standard pairs, all in implicit scope:

      ```tabular
      column  Marker
      column  Value types
      row  `Whatwg.Textual`  `Text`, `String`
      row  `Whatwg.Presence`, `Whatwg.Switch`, `Whatwg.Truth`  `Boolean`
      row  `Whatwg.Integral`, `Whatwg.PositiveInt`  `Int`
      row  `Whatwg.Decimal`  `Double`
      row  `Whatwg.Id`  `Name[DomId]`
      row  `Whatwg.CssClassList`  `Name[CssClass]`, `List[Name[CssClass]]`, `ClassList`
      row  `Whatwg.Ids`  `List[Name[DomId]]`
      row  `Whatwg.Url`  any typed URL
      row  `Whatwg.Css`  `Text`
      ```

      Other libraries add their own: graffiti's `urlText` lets a `Text` stand as a `Url`, and
      querencia's `scripting` lets a foreign DOM expression stand as an event handler.

      The set is not yet complete — many markers (`Target`, `Method`, `Sandbox`, `Rel`, …)
      have no given of their own, so the enums which name their values are passed as
      `.show` — and the choice given `attributives.textAttributive` makes `Text` acceptable
      for every marker at once, which is the usual import while it is being filled.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A URL for `src`, an `Int` for `width`, a `Boolean` for `ismap`
        code
            import soundness.*
            import htmlDoms.whatwg
            import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

            Img(src = url"https://example.com/a.png", width = 64, ismap = true).show
            // <img src="https://example.com/a.png" width="64" ismap>
      frame
        label A `false` `Boolean` omits the attribute
        code
            Img(src = url"https://example.com/a.png", ismap = false).show
            // <img src="https://example.com/a.png">
      frame
        label A `Text` where a URL is expected needs the choice given
        code
            Img(src = t"a.png")   // does not compile

            locally:
              import attributives.textAttributive
              Img(src = t"a.png").show   // <img src="a.png">
      ##
      ```
  refer honeycomb.Unattributive
  refer honeycomb.Attribute
  entry .textTextual   a `Text` as a `Whatwg.Textual` attribute value, as it is
  entry .stringTextual   a `String` as a `Whatwg.Textual` attribute value, as it is
  entry .boolean   a `Boolean` as a `Whatwg.Presence` attribute: present when true, omitted when false
  entry .switch   a `Boolean` as a `Whatwg.Switch` attribute: `on` or `off`
  entry .truth   a `Boolean` as a `Whatwg.Truth` attribute: `true` or `false`
  entry .int   an `Int` as a `Whatwg.Integral` attribute
  entry .posInt   an `Int` as a `Whatwg.PositiveInt` attribute; the sign is not checked
  entry .double   a `Double` as a `Whatwg.Decimal` attribute
  entry .domId   a `Name[DomId]` as a `Whatwg.Id` attribute
  entry .cssClass   a `Name[CssClass]` as a `Whatwg.CssClassList` attribute
  entry .classList   a `ClassList` as a `Whatwg.CssClassList` attribute, space-joined; empty omits it
  entry .url   any value `Abstractable across Urls to Text` as a `Whatwg.Url` attribute
      An `HttpUrl`, or any typed URL, written as its generic text. A plain `Text` is not
      one; `attributives.textAttributive` or graffiti's `urlText` admits it.
  entry .style   a `Text` as a `Whatwg.Css` attribute, as it is
  entry .cssClassList   a `List[Name[CssClass]]` as a `Whatwg.CssClassList` attribute, space-joined; empty omits it
  entry .domIds   a `List[Name[DomId]]` as a `Whatwg.Ids` attribute, space-joined; empty omits it
  entry #attribute   the name and text of the attribute for a value, the name alone, or `Unset` to omit it

entry .Unattributive   how an attribute's text is read back as a typed value, by its value marker
  detail
      The inverse of `Attributive`: `marker is Unattributive to result` converts the
      `Optional[Text]` an element holds for an attribute into a value, and is what
      `element.alt` or `element.width` on an `Html.Element` resolves through — the attribute
      given's `Topic` chooses the instance, so `Img(width = 50).width` is an `Optional[Int]`
      and `Img(alt = t"x").alt` a `Text`. Only four givens exist, so an attribute of any other
      marker cannot be read back by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
      import attributives.textAttributive

      Img(alt = t"hello world").alt   // t"hello world"
      Img(width = 50).width           // 50
      ```
  refer honeycomb.Attributive
  entry .textTextual   a `Whatwg.Textual` attribute as a `Text`, empty if absent
  entry .int   a `Whatwg.Integral` attribute as an `Optional[Int]`; `Unset` if absent or not a number
  entry .positiveInt   a `Whatwg.PositiveInt` attribute as an `Optional[Int]`; `Unset` if absent or not a number
  entry .cssClassList   a `Whatwg.CssClassList` attribute as a `List[Text]`, split on spaces; `Nil` if absent
  entry #unattribute   the value for an attribute's text, or for its absence

entry .ClassList   a set of CSS class names, for an element's `class` attribute
  detail
      `ClassList["one" | "two"]()` is a set of class names checked as the code compiles:
      each literal of the type argument must be a CSS identifier, so `ClassList["1bad"]()` is
      a compile error, _`1bad` is not a valid CSS class name_, rather than a runtime
      failure. The result has the refined type `ClassList of "one" | "two"`, and
      `ClassList(set)` wraps names already checked as `Name[CssClass]`.

      A class list is an attribute value through `Attributive.classList` — an empty one omits
      the attribute — and lists combine with `+` and `-`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      val base = ClassList["card" | "shadow"]()
      val active = base + ClassList["active"]()
      Div(`class` = active)(P("x")).show      // <div class="card shadow active"><p>x</p></div>
      Div(`class` = base - base)(P("x")).show // <div><p>x</p></div>
      ```
  refer honeycomb.Attribution
  refer honeycomb.Attributive
  entry .apply   `ClassList["a" | "b"]()`: a class list from literals, each checked as a CSS identifier
      A macro: the type argument must be a string literal or a union of them, or the
      expansion halts with _the class names should be string literals_; each literal is
      checked against `CssClass`'s `Nominative` rules. The case-class `apply(classes)` takes
      a `Set[Name[CssClass]]` already checked.
  entry .focusable   how a class list is named in a diagnostic: as a CSS selector, `.a.b`
      Given for every refined `ClassList of …` type, so a browser driver can locate elements
      by their classes.
  entry .addable   `list + other`: the union of two class lists
  entry .subtractable   `list - other`: the classes of one not in the other
  entry .empty   the empty class list, as the `Attribution` of the plain call
      A `ClassList` is `Topical`, and this given with `Topic = "apply"` serves the same role
      as `Attribution.empty` where a `ClassList` is the evidence.
  entry #classes   the names, as a `Set[Name[CssClass]]`

entry .Dom   a description of an HTML dialect: its tags, attributes, entities, doctype and inference rules
  detail
      The contract `Whatwg` and `Html4Transitional` implement, and the contextual value
      the parser, serializer and `h"…"` interpolator need: a `Dom` given — imported by
      name from `htmlDoms` (`htmlDoms.whatwg` or `htmlDoms.html4Transitional`), since
      `import soundness.*` brings in neither — says which tags exist, which attributes each
      admits, which named entities decode, what doctype a document gets, and which elements
      the parser may insert unwritten. A `Document[Html]` records the dom it was read under
      in its `Html.Provenance`.

      Another dialect is a class extending `Dom` with a `val` per tag made by `Tag.void`,
      `Tag.container` and `Tag.transparent`, and a companion of `Attribute` givens; the
      `elements` and `attributes` tables can be built from the members by reflection, as the
      two provided dialects do.
  refer honeycomb.Whatwg
  refer honeycomb.Html4Transitional
  refer honeycomb.Tag
  entry #elements   every tag of the dialect, by name
  entry #attributes   every attribute of the dialect, by name
  entry #entities   the named character references, name to replacement text
  entry #doctype   the doctype a `Document[Html]` of this dialect carries
  entry #infer   the tag to insert between a parent and a child it cannot hold directly, or `Unset`
      How `<table><tr>` gains its `<tbody>` and a bare `<p>` its `<html>` and `<body>` when
      read as a whole document.
  entry #generic   a root tag admitting every element of the dialect
      `Tag.root` over all of `#elements`' names: what `read[Html]` with no label parses
      into, so that a fragment may begin with any element.

entry .Renderable   how a value of any type becomes HTML, and what HTML it becomes
  detail
      A `value is Renderable in label` says that `value` renders to an `Html of label`, and
      lets it stand wherever that label is admitted: as a child of a tag, substituted into
      an `h"…"` literal, or converted with the `html` extension. The `Form` type member is the
      label (or union), so the compiler knows a `List[Text] is Renderable in "li"` may go in
      a `<ul>` and not in a `<div>`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      given (List[Text] is Renderable in "li") = list => Html.Fragment(list.map(Li(_))*)

      val items = List(t"one", t"two", t"three")
      Ul(items)                 // a <ul> of three <li>s
      h"<ol>$items</ol>"
      items.html
      ```

      Other libraries give their types a rendering: a `Message` and a `StackTrace` here;
      punctuation's Markdown, archimedes's `Math`, legerdemain's form fields, and graffiti's
      `Archetype` pages elsewhere, which is how a page is served as HTML with no glue.
  refer honeycomb.html
  refer honeycomb.Tag
  entry .message   a `Message` as phrasing content: its text, with nested messages rendered in turn
      `Form = Phrasing`, so a message may go in a `<p>` or a heading;
      `H4(stackTrace.message.html)` is how the stack-trace rendering uses it.
  entry .stackTrace   a `StackTrace` as flow content: a `<div class="stack">` with headings and a table of frames
      The component, class name and message as `<h2>`, `<h3>` and `<h4>`, then a `<table>`
      with a `<tr>` per frame, each cell classed `class`, `method`, `file`, `line` or `code`,
      for a stylesheet to pick out.
  entry #render   the HTML for a value, as an `Html of Form`

entry .Autocomplete   the values of `autocomplete`: `On` or `Off`
  detail
      `Autocomplete.On.show` is `on`. The attribute itself is typed `Whatwg.Switch`, which a
      `Boolean` satisfies directly through `Attributive.switch`; the enum has no
      `Attributive` of its own.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its lowercase name

entry .Capture   the values of an `<input type="file">`'s `capture`: `Environment` or `User`
  detail
      Shown lowercase. No `capture` attribute given is declared in the WHATWG vocabulary,
      so the enum is currently only a vocabulary of names.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its lowercase name

entry .Crossorigin   the values of `crossorigin`: `UseCredentials` or `Anonymous`
  detail
      Shown in kebab case, `use-credentials` and `anonymous`. The attribute is typed
      `Whatwg.Crossorigin`, for which the enum has no `Attributive`, so it is passed as
      `crossorigin = Crossorigin.Anonymous.show` with `attributives.textAttributive` in
      scope.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its kebab-cased name

entry .HDir   the values of `dir`: `Ltr`, `Rtl` or `Auto`
  detail
      The one attribute enum with an `Attributive` of its own, so `Body(dir = HDir.Rtl)`
      compiles with no import and writes `dir="rtl"`. Named `HDir` rather than `Dir` to
      leave room for the HTML 4 `<dir>` tag and other libraries' `Dir`.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its lowercase name
  entry .attributive   an `HDir` as a `Whatwg.Dir` attribute value

entry .HttpEquiv   the values of a `<meta>`'s `http-equiv`: `ContentSecurityPolicy`, `ContentType`, `DefaultStyle` or `Refresh`
  detail
      Shown in kebab case (`content-security-policy`). Passed as `.show` with
      `attributives.textAttributive`, since the enum has no `Attributive`.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its kebab-cased name

entry .Kind   the values of a `<track>`'s `kind`: `Subtitles`, `Captions`, `Descriptions`, `Chapters` or `Metadata`
  detail
      Shown lowercase. `Whatwg.Track` offers each as a preset tag (`Track.Captions`), which
      is the usual way to write one; the enum has no `Attributive`.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its lowercase name

entry .Method   the values of a `<form>`'s `method`: `Post`, `Get` or `Dialog`
  detail
      Shown lowercase. Passed as `method = Method.Post.show` with
      `attributives.textAttributive`, since the enum has no `Attributive`.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its lowercase name

entry .Preload   the values of `preload`: `None`, `Metadata` or `Auto`
  detail
      Shown lowercase. Written in full, `Preload.None`, so as not to be confused with
      `scala.None`. Passed as `.show`; the enum has no `Attributive`.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its lowercase name

entry .Rel   the link types of `rel`, from `Alternate` to `Tag`
  detail
      `Alternate`, `Author`, `Bookmark`, `Canonical`, `DnsPrefetch`, `External`, `Help`,
      `Icon`, `License`, `Manifest`, `Modulepreload`, `Next`, `Nofollow`, `Noopener`,
      `Noreferrer`, `Opener`, `Pingback`, `Preconnect`, `Prefetch`, `Preload`, `Prerender`,
      `Prev`, `Search`, `Stylesheet` and `Tag`, shown in kebab case (`dns-prefetch`). For a
      `<link>`, `Whatwg.Link` offers most as preset tags; on an `<a>`, pass `.show`.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its kebab-cased name

entry .Rev   the HTML 4 reverse link types of `rev`, from `Alternate` to `Bookmark`
  detail
      `Alternate`, `Stylesheet`, `Start`, `Next`, `Prev`, `Contents`, `Index`, `Glossary`,
      `Copyright`, `Chapter`, `Section`, `Subsection`, `Appendix`, `Help` and `Bookmark`,
      shown lowercase. `rev` exists only in `Html4Transitional`.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its lowercase name

entry .Sandbox   the tokens of an `<iframe>`'s `sandbox`
  detail
      `AllowForms`, `AllowPointerLock`, `AllowPopups`, `AllowPresentation`,
      `AllowSameOrigin`, `AllowScripts` and `AllowTopNavigation`, shown in kebab case
      (`allow-forms`). The attribute takes several, space-separated, as `Whatwg.Sandbox`;
      the enum has no `Attributive`, so a list is joined and passed as text.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its kebab-cased name

entry .Shape   the values of an `<area>`'s `shape`: `Circle`, `Default`, `Poly` or `Rect`
  detail
      Shown lowercase. `Whatwg.Area` offers each as a preset tag; the enum has no
      `Attributive`.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its lowercase name

entry .Target   the values of `target`: a named context, or `Self`, `Blank`, `Parent` or `Top`
  detail
      `Target.Browse(name)` is a named browsing context, shown as the name; the four
      keywords show as `_self`, `_blank`, `_parent` and `_top`. The attribute is typed
      `Whatwg.Target`, for which the enum has no `Attributive`, so it is passed as `.show`
      with `attributives.textAttributive` in scope.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its keyword or its context name

entry .Wrap   the values of a `<textarea>`'s `wrap`: `Soft` or `Hard`
  detail
      Shown lowercase. The WHATWG attribute is typed `Whatwg.Softness` and the HTML 4 one
      `Html4Transitional.Wrap` (any text); the enum has no `Attributive`, so it is passed
      as `.show`.
  entry .showable   renders a value as its lowercase name
